S_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nAny configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence not sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on some \\(H_j\\) iff \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2 Points not covered by the horizontals  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\), then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n\\(a\\le k\\). Consequently the set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side‑length \\(k\\); it contains\n\\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n#### 2.3 Sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\ny=i x+(n+1-2i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0\\) and \\(i\\neq-1\\), each\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny. The slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are distinct, so the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n*Claim.* Every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate\nis \\(a\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\). By the definition of \\(T_k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nn-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(b=n+1-a-t\\) with an integer \\(t\\ge 0\\). From the left inequality in (5) we\nobtain  \n\n\\[\nn+1-a-t\\ge n-k+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; t\\le k-a .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) into the equation of \\(L_a\\) (the line with\n\\(i=a\\)):\n\n\\[\ny = a x + (n+1-2a)\n   = a\\cdot a + n+1-2a\n   = n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nThus the equality \\(y=n+1-a\\) holds precisely when \\(t=0\\).  \nIf \\(t>0\\) we use the fact that the points of \\(L_a\\) with integer abscissa\n\\(x=a-t\\) have ordinate  \n\n\\[\ny = a(a-t)+(n+1-2a)=n+1-a-t .\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(x=a-t\\) (which is a positive integer because \\(t\\le k-a\\) and\n\\(a\\le k\\)) we obtain the point \\((a-t,\\,n+1-a-t)\\), which belongs to \\(T_k\\)\nand lies on \\(L_a\\). In particular, for the original point \\((a,b)\\) we have\n\\(b=n+1-a-t\\) with the same \\(t\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is exactly the point of\n\\(L_a\\) whose abscissa is \\(a\\). ∎\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nT_k\\subseteq \\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.4 The whole family of lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(n\\) distinct lines; the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\)\nare sunny, while the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are not.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) either satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\) and is therefore on some\n\\(H_j\\), or belongs to \\(T_k\\) and, by (7), lies on a suitable \\(L_i\\). Hence each\n\\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines. Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) this shows that the\nset of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
