er of sunny lines cannot exceed the number of columns that are allowed to be non‑vertical, i.e.\n\n\\[\ns\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every admissible \\(s\\)\n\nLet \\(s\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le s\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n1. **Vertical lines.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x_{v}=n-s .\n   \\]\n\n   They are \\(n-s\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and they cover completely the columns\n   \\(C_{1},C_{2},\\dots ,C_{n-s}\\).\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,s\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{j}:\\; y = j\\,x-(j-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{j}\\) equals \\(j\\neq0,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{j}\\) is sunny.\n   For a column \\(x=a\\) with \\(a>n-s\\) we obtain the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{j,a}= \\bigl(a,\\;j a-(j-1)\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\ge n-s+1\\) and \\(j\\le s\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\),\n\n   \\[\n   1\\le j a-(j-1)\\le n+1-a,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(P_{j,a}\\in T_n\\). For fixed \\(a\\) the points \\(P_{1,a},\\dots ,P_{s,a}\\) are distinct (different slopes give different \\(y\\)-coordinates). Hence each sunny line contributes exactly one point in every column \\(C_{a}\\) with \\(a>n-s\\).\n\n3. **Coverage of the remaining columns.**  \n   In column \\(C_{a}\\) with \\(a>n-s\\) the total number of points is \\(n+1-a\\). By the choice of \\(s\\),\n\n   \\[\n   s\\ge n+1-a,\n   \\]\n\n   therefore the \\(s\\) points supplied by the sunny lines are sufficient to cover the whole column (any surplus points are simply ignored).  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nThe family consists of \\(n-s\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(s\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines. Since the construction works for every integer \\(s\\) satisfying (3), the bound (3) i