------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nWrite \\(k\\) for the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are\nnon‑sunny.\n\nConsider the columns \\(C_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\) for \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\).\nColumn \\(C_a\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points.\n\nA sunny line can contribute **at most one** point to a given column,\nwhereas a vertical line contributes **all** points of that column.\nHence, to cover column \\(C_a\\) we need either the vertical line \\(x=a\\) or at\nleast \\(n+1-a\\) sunny lines (one for each point of the column).\n\nIf we omit the vertical line \\(x=a\\), we must have at least \\(n+1-a\\) sunny\nlines.  Since the total number of sunny lines is \\(k\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n+1-a\\qquad\\text{whenever the line }x=a\\text{ is absent}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(m\\) be the largest integer such that the vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) are present.  For the next column \\(a=m+1\\) we either keep the\nvertical line \\(x=m+1\\) or, by (6), we need \\(k\\ge n-m\\) sunny lines.\nBecause \\(k\\le n\\), the inequality \\(k\\ge n-m\\) can hold only when  \n\n\\[\nn-m\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(m\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently at least \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) vertical lines are forced,\nand the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the number of columns that are\nallowed to be non‑vertical, namely  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nSet  \n\n\\[\nv=n-2k\\qquad(\\text{note that }v\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil).\n\\]\n\n**(i) Vertical lines.**  \nTake the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=v .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover completely the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{v}\\).\n\n**(ii) Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{j}:\\; y=j\\bigl(x-(v+1)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{j}\\) equals \\(j\\neq0,-1\\); hence each \\(\\ell_{j}\\) is sunny.\n\nFor a column \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge v+1\\) the intersection point is  \n\n\\[\nP_{j,a}= \\bigl(a,\\;j(a-(v+1))+1\\bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge v+1\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &\\le j(a-(v+1))+1,\\\\\nj(a-(v+1))+1 &\\le k\\bigl(a-(v+1)\\bigr)+1\n               \\le k\\bigl(n-(v+1)\\bigr)+1\n               = k(2k-1)+1 \\le n+1-a .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The last inequality uses \\(a\\ge v+1=n-2k+1\\) and the hypothesis\n\\(k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).)  Hence \\(P_{j,a}\\in T_n\\).\n\nFor a fixed column \\(a\\) the points \\(\\{P_{j,a}\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) are distinct,\nso each sunny line contributes exactly one point in every column\n\\(C_{a}\\) with \\(a\\ge v+1\\).\n\n**(iii) Coverage.**  \n\n* Columns \\(1,\\dots ,v\\) are completely covered by the vertical lines (8).  \n* For a column \\(a\\ge v+1\\) the number of points that have to be covered is\n  \\(|C_{a}|=n+1-a\\).  Since \\(k\\ge n+1-a\\) (because \\(a\\ge v+1=n-2k+1\\)),\n  the \\(k\\) points supplied by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\)\n  are sufficient to cover the whole column.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\nThe family consists of \\(v=n-2k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny\nlines, i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7); for \\(k=0\\) we\nobtain the pure vertical family \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\).\n\