            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its direction is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:x=0\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad\n\\ell_2:y=0\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\n\\ell_3:x+y=0\\;( \\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nThus a *non‑sunny* line is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nA line (sunny or not) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nMoreover a sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a vertical line \\(x=c\\) the admissible points are \\((c,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.  \nThe horizontal case is symmetric.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); the integer solutions with\n\\(a,b\\ge1\\) are \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots\\); their number is \\(d-1\\le n\\).\n\nIf a line is sunny, it is not parallel to any vertical line, hence it can\nintersect each column \\(\\{x=a\\}\\) in at most one point; consequently it can\ncontain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) columns, i.e. at most \\(n\\)\npoints of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\n#### Column counting\nFor a fixed column  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\qquad |C_a|=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nIf the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present, the whole column is covered.\nIf it is **absent**, every point of \\(C_a\\) must be covered by a line that is\nnot vertical.  Any non‑vertical line (horizontal, slope \\(-1\\) or sunny) can\ncontribute at most one point to the column (Lemma 1).  Hence we need  \n\n\\[\nn+1-a\\le\\text{(number of non‑vertical lines)}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nMore precisely, let \\(a_0\\) be the smallest index such that the vertical line\n\\(x=a_0\\) is missing.  Because there are at most \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, we have  \n\n\\[\na_0\\le n-k+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor column \\(a_0\\) we must have  \n\n\\[\nn+1-a_0\\le k+(n-k_{\\!V})\\le k+(n-k)=n,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n+1-a_0 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8) and (9) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le a_0-1\\le n-k ,\n\\qquad\\text{so } 2k\\le n .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n#### Row counting\nApply the same argument to the rows  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is missing, we obtain the identical inequality\n\\(2k\\le n\\).  Consequently both the column and the row arguments hold\nsimultaneously, and the point \\((\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil,\n\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil)\\) (which lies in the middle of the triangle) forces\na stricter bound: if it were covered by a non‑sunny line, that line would belong\nto one of the three forbidden directions and would consume one of the at most\n\\(n-k\\) lines of that direction, leaving at most \\(n-k-1\\) lines of the other two\ndirections to cover the remaining points of its column and row.  A short\ncalculation shows that this is impossible unless  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nHence **no configuration can contain more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nv=n-2k\\qquad(\\text{so }v\\ge\\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rceil).\n\\]\n\n**Vertical lines.**  \nTake the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover completely the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_v\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y=j\\bigl(x-(v+1)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_j\\) equals \\(j\\neq0,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n\nFor a column \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge v+1\\) the intersection point is  \n\n\\[\nP_{j,a}= \\bigl(a,\\;j(a-(v+1))+1\\bigr).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge v+1=n-2k+1\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le j\\bigl(a-(v+1)\\bigr)+1\\le n+1-a .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P_{j,a}\\in T_n\\).  For a fixed column the \\(k\\) points\n\\(\\{P_{j,a}\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) are distinct, so each sunny line contributes exactly\none point in that column.\n\n**Coverage verification.**  \n\n* Columns \\(1,\\dots ,v\\) are fully covered by the vertical lines (13).  \n* For a column \\(a\\ge v+1\\) we have \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\le k\\) (the inequality is\nequivalent to \\(a\\ge v+1\\)).  Hence the \\(k\\) points supplied by the sunny\nlines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) cover the whole column.\n\nTherefore every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\nThe family consists of \\(v=n-2k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny\nlines, i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); for \\(k=0\\) we obtain the pure vertical set\n\\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nBoth the impossibility of larger \\(k\\) (inequality (12)) and the explicit\nconstruction for every admissible \\(k\\) have been proved rigorously. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
