ma y+\\delta ,\\qquad\\delta:=1-\\gamma .\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\((1,1)\\in V\\). As in step 2 we select \\(\\gamma=\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\); this choice guarantees that \\((1,n)\\) also lies on \\(V\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   x=\\gamma n+\\delta\n     =\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}+1-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the whole set \\(V_1\\) is contained in \\(V\\), and \\(V\\) is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) nor infinite).  \n   Replacing \\(x=1\\) by \\(V\\) therefore preserves the covering, and now **all** \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Steps 4a and 4b show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A covering without sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1),\\qquad L_n:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(b\\le n\\),\n\n* if \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_n\\);\n* if \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_b\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nAll these lines are either horizontal or vertical, hence none of them is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A sunny line covering a whole horizontal segment)\n\nFix \\(s\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_s:=\\frac{1}{\\,n-s\\,}\\qquad(\\text{a rational number}),\n\\qquad\n\\beta_s:=s-\\alpha_s .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_s:\\; y=\\alpha_s x+\\beta_s .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\alpha_s\\neq0\\) and \\(\\alpha_s\\neq-1\\) (the denominator \\(n-s\\ge1\\) and \\(\\alpha_s>0\\)), the line \\(S_s\\) is sunny.\n\nWe have  \n\n\\[\ny=\\alpha_s\\cdot1+\\beta_s =\\alpha_s+s-\\alpha_s =s,\n\\]\n\nso \\((1,s)\\in S_s\\).  \nSimilarly,\n\n\\[\ny=\\alpha_s\\,(n+1-s)+\\beta_s\n   =\\alpha_s\\,(n+1-s)+s-\\alpha_s\n   =\\alpha_s\\,(n-s)+s\n   =s,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\alpha_s\\,(n-s)=\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-s\\,}(n-s)=1\\).  \nHence \\((n+1-s,s)\\in S_s\\) as well.\n\nThe two points \\((1,s)\\) and \\((n+1-s,s)\\) are distinct, so the line determined by them is unique; consequently **every** point of the horizontal segment  \n\n\\[\nH_s:=\\{(a,s)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-s\\}\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(S_s\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing a horizontal line preserves the covering)\n\nLet \\(C\\) be the family of lines from Lemma 1.  \nFix \\(s\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nC' = \\bigl(C\\setminus\\{L_s\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_s\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(S_s\\) is the sunny line from Lemma 2.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate is different from \\(s\\) are still covered, because the corresponding horizontal line \\(L_b\\) (or the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) remains in \\(C'\\).  \n\nThe points with second coordinate equal to \\(s\\) form exactly the set \\(H_s\\); by Lemma 2 we have \\(H_s\\subseteq S_s\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by a line of \\(C'\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (A sunny line covering the whole vertical segment)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\gamma:=\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,},\\qquad\n\\delta:=1-\\gamma .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=\\gamma y+\\delta .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\gamma>0\\) and \\(\\gamma\\neq1\\), the line \\(V\\) is neither vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\); hence it is sunny.\n\nFor \\(y=1\\) we obtain \\(x=\\gamma+\\delta=1\\); for \\(y=n\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx=\\gamma n+\\delta\n   =\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}+1-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}=1 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the whole set  \n\n\\[\nV_1:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nis contained in \\(V\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Realising any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(s\\in S\\) replace the horizontal line \\(L_s\\) by the sunny line \\(S_s\\) of Lemma 2.  \nBy Lemma 3 the covering is preserved.  \nExactly the lines indexed by \\(S\\) are sunny, so the resulting family has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nFirst perform the replacements described above for **all** \\(s=1,\\dots ,n-1\\); we now have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and the vertical line \\(L_n:x=1\\).  \nReplace \\(L_n\\) by the sunny line \\(V\\) of Lemma 4.  \nSince \\(V\\) contains the whole set \\(V_1\\), the covering of \\(T_n\\) is still valid.  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are now sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 5 shows that each integer \\(k\\) in this set can be realised.  \nConversely, any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no other values of \\(k\\) are possible. ∎"
