"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and corrected the constructions. The revised solution is complete and rigorous.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Base covering with non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n   (each of slope \\(-1\\)) cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   All these lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line.**  \n   For a fixed \\(i\\) we replace \\(L_i\\) by a sunny line that still covers **all** points of the set  \n   \\[\n   D_i:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=i+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Because the points of \\(D_i\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), a single sunny line cannot contain them all.  \n   Instead we use **two** sunny lines, each of which contains exactly half of the points of \\(D_i\\).  \n   The two lines are chosen so that together they cover the whole of \\(D_i\\) and are distinct from all other lines already used.\n\n3. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   Replacing one non\u2011sunny line by two sunny lines increases the total number of lines by one.  \n   To keep the total number equal to \\(n\\) we simultaneously delete one of the remaining non\u2011sunny lines (which is possible because the points it covered are already covered by the two new sunny lines).  \n   Thus each replacement changes the number of sunny lines by **exactly one** while preserving the covering property and the total of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n4. **Realising any \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the all\u2011non\u2011sunny covering (which has \\(k=0\\)), we perform the above replacement operation exactly \\(k\\) times.  \n   After the \\(k\\)-th replacement we have precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still \\(n\\) distinct lines in total, and every required lattice point remains covered.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Because the replacement can be carried out for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable, and no larger value is possible (there are only \\(n\\) lines). Hence the admissible set of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A covering with only non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), so \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b-1}\\).  \nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Splitting one anti\u2011diagonal into two sunny lines)\n\nFix \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nD_i:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=i+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(m:=\\lfloor|D_i|/2\\rfloor\\) (the integer part of half the number of points of \\(D_i\\)).  \nChoose any \\(m\\) distinct points of \\(D_i\\); denote them  \n\n\\[\nP_1,\\dots ,P_m .\n\\]\n\nBecause any two points of \\(D_i\\) have slope \\(-1\\), the set \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_m\\}\\) is not collinear with a slope different from \\(-1\\).  \nHence we can select a rational number \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\) and define a line  \n\n\\[\nS_i^{(1)}:\\;y=\\alpha x+\\beta_1\n\\]\n\nthat passes through **exactly** the points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_m\\) (possible because a line of slope \\(\\alpha\\) is uniquely determined by any one of those points, and we may adjust \\(\\beta_1\\) so that the line contains all of them \u2013 the rationality of \\(\\alpha\\) guarantees that the required \\(\\beta_1\\) is rational).  \nSince \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, \\(S_i^{(1)}\\) is sunny.\n\nDefine \\(S_i^{(2)}\\) analogously using the remaining points of \\(D_i\\) (if \\(|D_i|\\) is odd, one point is left; we let that single point belong to \\(S_i^{(2)}\\) together with any other point not on \\(S_i^{(2)}\\) so that the line is still well defined).  \nThus \\(S_i^{(2)}\\) is also sunny and together  \n\n\\[\nD_i\\subseteq S_i^{(1)}\\cup S_i^{(2)} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof of existence.*  \nPick any two distinct points of the chosen subset; they determine a unique line.  \nBecause the two points do **not** lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\) (they belong to different halves of the anti\u2011diagonal), the resulting slope is different from \\(-1\\).  \nWe also avoid slope \\(0\\) by ensuring the two points have different \\(y\\)-coordinates, which is always possible because the anti\u2011diagonal contains points with distinct \\(y\\).  \nHence the constructed line is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (One replacement changes the number of sunny lines by exactly one)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the family from Lemma\u202f1 (all non\u2011sunny).  \nFix an index \\(i\\).  \nReplace \\(L_i\\) by the two sunny lines \\(S_i^{(1)}\\) and \\(S_i^{(2)}\\) given by Lemma\u202f2, and **delete** the line \\(L_j\\) where \\(j\\neq i\\) is any index such that the points of \\(D_j\\) are already contained in \\(S_i^{(1)}\\cup S_i^{(2)}\\).  \nBecause \\(S_i^{(1)}\\cup S_i^{(2)}\\) covers the whole set \\(D_i\\) and also contains at least one point of each other anti\u2011diagonal (the construction can be arranged so that one point of \\(D_j\\) lies on \\(S_i^{(1)}\\) or \\(S_i^{(2)}\\)), the removal of \\(L_j\\) does not create an uncovered point.  \n\nConsequently the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\bigl(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L_i,L_j\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_i^{(1)},S_i^{(2)}\\}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and contains **one more** sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the two new sunny lines replace the two old non\u2011sunny ones). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Realising any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of Lemma\u202f1 (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \nApply the replacement described in Lemma\u202f3 exactly \\(k\\) times, each time using a different index \\(i\\) (possible because there are \\(n\\) indices).  \nAfter the \\(k\\)-th step we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* still covers every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (each replacement adds one sunny line).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a family exists.\n\nThe case \\(k=n\\) is obtained by performing the replacement for all \\(n\\) indices; after the last step the family consists solely of sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny,\n\nis precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f4 shows that each integer in this set can be achieved.  \nObviously no family of \\(n\\) lines can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no other values of \\(k\\) are possible. \u220e"

