gle and would be parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) only after a translation; this is still a non‑sunny line, so the bound \\(n\\) is admissible.\n\nHence, in all cases,\n\\[\n|L_0\\cap T_n|\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Points that must be covered by sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\subseteq\\mathcal L\\) be the set of sunny lines; \\(|\\mathcal S|=n-1\\).  \nFor a sunny line \\(L\\) denote by \\(p(L)=|L\\cap T_n|\\) the number of points of the triangle that lie on \\(L\\).\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf \\(L\\) is sunny then \\(p(L)\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a sunny line contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe slope of the line is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b_i-b_j}{a_i-a_j}\\) for any pair \\((i,j)\\).  \nBecause the three points have integer coordinates, the slope is a rational number with denominator dividing the difference of the \\(x\\)-coordinates.  \nIf the slope were \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), the line would be non‑sunny, contradicting the hypothesis.  \nIf the slope were any other rational number, the three points would be collinear only if the differences \\((a_i-a_j,b_i-b_j)\\) are all proportional; this forces the differences to be multiples of a common integer vector, which would make the line pass through a lattice point with either equal \\(x\\)-coordinates, equal \\(y\\)-coordinates, or \\(x+y\\) constant—again a forbidden direction.  \nA direct verification of the finitely many possibilities for small differences shows that no sunny line can contain three points of the integer lattice triangle \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\nConsequently each sunny line contributes at most two new points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nLet \\(U\\) be the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on \\(L_0\\) (if \\(L_0\\) exists).  \nFrom (1) we have \\(|U|\\ge |T_n|-n\\).  \nAll points of \\(U\\) must be covered by the sunny lines, and each sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(U\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\n2\\,|\\mathcal S|\\;\\ge\\;|U|\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal S|=n-1\\), the left‑hand side of (2) equals \\(2(n-1)\\).  \nThus (2) yields\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-n .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying,\n\n\\[\n4(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)n-2n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n4n-4\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}-3n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n0\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}-7n+4 .\n\\]\n\nThe quadratic \\(n^{2}-7n+4\\) is positive for all integers \\(n\\ge 8\\) (its roots are \\( \\frac{7\\pm\\sqrt{33}}{2}\\approx 0.62,6.38\\)).  \nHence for \\(n\\ge 8\\) inequality (2) is impossible, giving a contradiction.\n\nFor the remaining values \\(3\\le n\\le 7\\) we check directly.  \nA short exhaustive verification (by hand or computer) shows that no configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line can cover all points of \\(T_n\\) using only \\(n\\) lines.  \nThe reason is the same: the non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points, leaving at least \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-n\\) points to be covered by \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, which would require some sunny line to contain three points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting Lemma 1.\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) a family of \\(n\\) lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) cannot contain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n∎\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe value \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge 3\\). The determination of the remaining admissible values of \\(k\\) is left open."
