al lines would be required to cover the remaining \\(n-2\\) column points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(1,n-1)\\).  \n   Hence **at least two non\u2011sunny lines are unavoidable**.  \n   Since the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n-2\\).\n\n5. **Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)**  \n\n   *Base non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Keep the two lines from (1):\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines that also cover the interior points.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{t+2}:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t-1}\\,x-\\frac{1}{t-1}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\dfrac{t}{t-1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(L_{t+2}\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le t\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{t}{t-1}a-\\frac{1}{t-1}=t+1-a,\n   \\]\n   so  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,\\;t+1-a)\\in L_{t+2}\\qquad (2\\le a\\le t).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(L_{t+2}\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S_{n}\\) lying on the diagonal\n   \\[\n   a+b=t+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Family with a prescribed number of sunny lines.*  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{k}:=\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\cup\\{L_{t+2}\\mid 2\\le t\\le k\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) consists of \\(2+k\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   *Verification that \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\). Then \\(r:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le r\\le n+1\\).\n\n   * If \\(r=2\\) then \\((a,b)=(1,1)\\) lies on both \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\).  \n   * If \\(r\\ge 3\\) write \\(t=r-1\\). Since \\(3\\le r\\le n+1\\), we have \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).  \n     When \\(t\\le k\\) the line \\(L_{t+2}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\), and by (4) the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to that line.  \n     When \\(t>k\\) we 