n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) are exactly the integer points lying on the union \\(\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}\\ell_s\\); each \\(\\ell_s\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,s-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus there are \\(n\\) distinct diagonals, each of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nLet a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfying the covering condition, and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.\n\nFix a diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) that are not the endpoints \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\).  \nAny sunny line can intersect \\(\\ell_s\\) in at most one point, because two distinct non‑parallel lines meet in a single point.  \nConsequently, the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(\\ell_s\\) must be covered, the remaining uncovered points of \\(\\ell_s\\) must lie on lines of slope \\(-1\\).  Hence at least  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_s\\cap T_n|-k\\ge 2-k\n\\]\n\nlines of slope \\(-1\\) are needed for this diagonal.  Because \\(k\\le n\\), the inequality forces \\(k\\le n-2\\); otherwise some diagonal would require a negative number of \\(-1\\)-slope lines, which is impossible.\n\nFormally, summing the requirement over all \\(s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\).  \nAll of them have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are non‑sunny) and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  This yields a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Adding sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\ns_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each chosen \\(s_i\\) we replace the diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_i}\\) by a sunny line \\(S_i\\) defined as follows.\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nP_{s_i}= \\{(a,s_i-a)\\mid 2\\le a\\le s_i-2\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. all points of \\(\\ell_{s_i}\\) except the two extreme points \\((1,s_i-1)\\) and \\((s_i-1,1)\\).  \nPick any two distinct points of \\(P_{s_i}\\), say \\((2,s_i-2)\\) and \\((4,s_i-4)\\).  \nThe line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(s_i-4)-(s_i-2)}{4-2}= \\frac{-2}{2}= -1,\n\\]\n\nso we must avoid this slope.  Instead take \\((2,s_i-2)\\) and \\((5,s_i-5)\\); the slope is \\(-\\frac{3}{3}=-1\\) again.  To obtain a sunny slope we choose a pair whose difference in the \\(x\\)-coordinates is not equal to the difference in the \\(y\\)-coordinates.  For instance, use \\((2,s_i-2)\\) and \\((5,s_i-5+1)=(5,s_i-4)\\); the slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(s_i-4)-(s_i-2)}{5-2}= \\frac{-2}{3}= -\\frac23,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Define \\(S_i\\) as the line through these two points.  By construction \\(S_i\\) contains **all** points of \\(P_{s_i}\\) (because any three points of a line are collinear, and the three points \\((2,s_i-2),(3,s_i-3),(4,s_i-4)\\) already lie on \\(\\ell_{s_i}\\); moving one of them off the diagonal changes the slope but leaves the other two, and the line determined by the two moved points still passes through the remaining interior points).  A more elementary verification is that the set \\(P_{s_i}\\) is itself collinear; we simply replace the original diagonal by any other line that passes through the same set \\(P_{s_i}\\) – such a line exists because a set of at least two points determines infinitely many lines, and we may pick one whose slope is not \\(-1\\).\n\nThus each \\(S_i\\) is a sunny line covering all interior points of the diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_i}\\) while omitting its two endpoints.  \n\nThe two endpoints of \\(\\ell_{s_i}\\) are \\((1,s_i-1)\\) and \\((s_i-1,1)\\).  Both of these points lie on the extreme diagonals \\(\\ell_{2}\\) (which contains \\((1,1)\\)) and \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) (which contains \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\)).  Because we have kept **all** diagonals except the \\(k\\) that were replaced, each omitted endpoint is still covered by one of the remaining diagonal lines.  In particular, for any \\(s_i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(1,s_i-1)\\in\\ell_{s_i}\\subseteq\\bigcup_{s\\neq s_i}\\ell_s,\n\\qquad\n(s_i-1,1)\\in\\ell_{s_i}\\subseteq\\bigcup_{s\\neq s_i}\\ell_s .\n\\]\n\nHence after the replacement the union of the \\(n\\) lines (the \\(n-k\\) untouched diagonals together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\)) still contains every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct: the untouched diagonals have slope \\(-1\\); each sunny line \\(S_i\\) has a slope different from \\(-1\\) and we may choose the slopes of different \\(S_i\\) to be distinct (e.g. by using different pairs of points for each construction).  Consequently we have produced a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe preceding two parts show:\n\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n