\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the slope of the diagonals).  \n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies (1) and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nFix a diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\).  The set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s\\cap T_n=\\{(a,s-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at least two points that are not the extreme points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\).  \nA sunny line can intersect \\(\\ell_s\\) in at most one point, because two distinct non\u2011parallel lines meet in at most one point.  Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\ell_s\\).  The remaining points of \\(\\ell_s\\) must therefore be covered by lines of slope \\(-1\\).  Since \\(\\ell_s\\) contains at least two points, at least two lines of slope \\(-1\\) are needed for each diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\).\n\nThere are \\(n-2\\) such diagonals, so the total number of lines of slope \\(-1\\) in the configuration is at least \\(2\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\).  All have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are non\u2011sunny) and by (1) they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  This yields a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\ns_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) we replace the diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_i}\\) by a sunny line \\(S_i\\) constructed as follows.\n\n*Define the interior set*  \n\n\\[\nP_{s_i}= \\{(a,s_i-a)\\mid 2\\le a\\le s_i-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s_i\\ge 3\\), the set \\(P_{s_i}\\) is non\u2011empty.  \nPick two distinct points of \\(P_{s_i}\\); we use  \n\n\\[\nQ_{i,1}=(2,s_i-2),\\qquad Q_{i,2}=(3,s_i-3).\n\\]\n\nChoose a third point that is **not** on the diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_i}\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(4,s_i-5).\n\\]\n\nThe three points \\(Q_{i,1},Q_{i,2},R_i\\) are not collinear (the slope of the line through \\(Q_{i,1},Q_{i,2}\\) is \\(-1\\) whereas the slope of the line through \\(Q_{i,1},R_i\\) is \\(-\\frac{3}{2}\\neq -1\\)).  Hence they determine a unique line, denote it by \\(S_i\\).\n\nThe line \\(S_i\\) contains the two points \\(Q_{i,1},Q_{i,2}\\); therefore it contains **all** points of \\(P_{s_i}\\) (any point of \\(P_{s_i}\\) lies on the line through \\(Q_{i,1}\\) and \\(Q_{i,2}\\)).  Its slope is \\(-\\frac{3}{2}\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe two extreme points of the original diagonal,\n\\((1,s_i-1)\\) and \\((s_i-1,1)\\), are not on \\(S_i\\).  They remain covered because we keep **all** the other diagonal lines unchanged; in particular each of them lies on the diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_i-1}\\), which is still present.\n\nAfter performing this replacement for every \\(i\\) we obtain a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\ell_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nProperties of \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n1. **Cardinality.**  There are \\(n-k\\) untouched diagonals and \\(k\\) new lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n2. **Number of sunny lines.**  The \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny, while every untouched diagonal has slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  Hence exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines appear.\n3. **Coverage of \