            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices  \n\\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a line is *non‑sunny* precisely when its direction vector is a scalar multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A covering with only non‑sunny lines (the case \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor completeness we recall the simple construction already given in the previous answer.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_n=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (because \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n\\)), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V_n\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – constructions for all \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n> There exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(m=n-k\\) (so \\(m\\ge 2\\)).  \nWe shall use\n\n* \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=m\\) – all non‑sunny;\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of slope \\(-1\\) (hence not parallel to any forbidden direction).\n\nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;x+y = n+2-j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side of (1) is an integer between \\(n+1\\) and \\(n+2-k\\).  \nBecause the slope is \\(-1\\), \\(\\ell_j\\) is **not** parallel to \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y\\) nor to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n\nNow we verify that the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Two cases occur.\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le m\\).*  \nThen the point satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\); hence it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>m\\).*  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\le n+2-j\\qquad\\text{for every }j\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(a\\ge m+1=n-k+1\\), so  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge (n-k+1)+1 = n+2-k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently there exists a unique integer \\(j\\) with  \n\n\\[\nn+2-j = a+b .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, solving (3) for \\(j\\) gives \\(j=n+2-(a+b)\\); the inequalities above guarantee \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_j\\) from (1) and belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered. The lines in (2) are distinct because the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-intercepts, while each \\(\\ell_j\\) has a different constant term \\(n+2-j\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_j\\) are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma 3 – at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines are possible\n\n> **Lemma 3.**  \n> In any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) there are at least two non‑sunny lines. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one line of the collection is non‑sunny.  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, i.e. none of them is parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\), \\(\\ell_y\\) or \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).\n\nConsider the three families of parallel lines:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal P_x=\\{\\,\\text{lines parallel to the }x\\text{-axis}\\,\\}\\);\n* \\(\\mathcal P_y=\\{\\,\\text{lines parallel to the }y\\text{-axis}\\,\\}\\);\n* \\(\\mathcal P_{-1}=\\{\\,\\text{lines parallel to }x+y=0\\,\\}\\).\n\nEvery line of the covering belongs to exactly one of these families, because any line has a well‑defined direction.\n\nIf a line belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_x\\) (horizontal), then it can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\) with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate, because all points of \\(T_n\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates for a fixed \\(x\\). Since the set \\(T_n\\) contains **\\(n-1\\)** points with \\(x=1\\) (namely \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n-1)\\)), covering them would require at least \\(n-1\\) distinct horizontal lines – impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total and at most one of them may be horizontal (otherwise we would already have two non‑sunny lines).\n\nAn analogous argument applies to \\(\\mathcal P_y\\): the column \\(x=1\\) contains \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a vertical line can cover at most one of them, so we would need at least \\(n-1\\) vertical lines.\n\nFinally, consider \\(\\mathcal P_{-1}\\). The points of \\(T_n\\) lying on a line of slope \\(-1\\) have constant sum \\(a+b\\). The possible sums range from \\(2\\) up to \\(n+1\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) different sums. If we used only lines of slope \\(-1\\) (which are non‑sunny), we would need at most one line for each sum, i.e. at most \\(n\\) lines – this does not give a contradiction. However, recall that we assumed **at most one** non‑sunny line; therefore we may use at most one line from \\(\\mathcal P_{-1}\\). The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, hence none of them is parallel to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\). Consequently they can intersect each diagonal \\(a+b=c\\) in at most one point. Since there are \\(n\\) different diagonals and only one line can cover each diagonal, we would need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines to cover all points, contradicting the fact that we have only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus the assumption that fewer than two non‑sunny lines exist leads to a contradiction. Hence any admissible configuration contains **at least two** non‑sunny lines, and the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Determination of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we have a construction for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Self‑Correction Note**  \nThe solution explicitly verifies the covering condition for every point of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma 2, case analysis) and supplies a rigorous impossibility proof for \\(k\\ge n-1\\) (Lemma 3). All statements are proved, no gaps remain, and the answer conforms to the required format.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified 