            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices  \n\\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a line is *non‑sunny* precisely when its direction vector is a scalar multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 2 – constructions for all \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n> There exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPut \\(m=n-k\\) (so \\(m\\ge 2\\)).  \n\n**Non‑sunny part.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n**Sunny part.**  \nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;x-y = j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(+1\\); therefore \\(S_j\\) is not parallel to \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y\\) nor to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) and is sunny.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\cup\\{S_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe lines in (2) are distinct because the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-intercepts and the sunny lines have different constants on the right‑hand side of (1).\n\nWe now verify that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le m\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\), i.e. it lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>m\\).*  \nSince \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nBecause \\(a>m=n-k\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\na+b \\ge (n-k+1)+1 = n+2-k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nn+2-k \\le a+b \\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus there exists a unique integer \\(j\\) with  \n\n\\[\nj = a-b \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-b = j,\\qquad 1\\le j\\le k .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the inequalities above give \\(1\\le a-b\\le k\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_{a-b}\\) from (1) and belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\nIn both cases the point is covered. Therefore \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfies (i) and contains exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 3 – at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines are possible\n\n> **Lemma 3.**  \n> In any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) there are at least two non‑sunny lines. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one of the \\(n\\) lines is non‑sunny.  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nConsider the **first column**\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\nand the **first row**\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\nBoth sets contain exactly \\(n-1\\) points, and \\(C\\cup R\\) contains \\(2(n-1)\\) points (the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both).\n\nA sunny line is not vertical, so it can intersect the column \\(C\\) in **at most one** point; otherwise two points of \\(C\\) would share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical.  \nSimilarly, a sunny line can intersect the row \\(R\\) in at most one point.\n\nTherefore each sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(C\\cup R\\) (one from \\(C\\) and one from \\(R\\)).  \nWith at most one non‑sunny line available, the total number of points of \\(C\\cup R\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n1\\cdot (n-1) \\;+\\; (n-1)\\cdot 2 \\;=\\; 2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|C\\cup R| = 2(n-1)=2n-2\\).  \nTo achieve the required covering we would need the non‑sunny line to cover **all** points of either \\(C\\) or \\(R\\); however a non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA vertical line can cover all of \\(C\\) but none of \\(R\\) except possibly \\((1,1)\\); a horizontal line can cover all of \\(R\\) but none of \\(C\\) except \\((1,1)\\); a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets each of \\(C\\) and \\(R\\) in at most one point.  \nIn any of these three possibilities the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would have to cover the other set of \\(n-1\\) points, but each sunny line contributes at most one point to that set, forcing the need for at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines **in addition** to the one already used for the first set. This would require at least \\(n\\) sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nHence our assumption is false: at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Determination of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLemma 2 provides, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 3 shows that no family can