) in **at most one** point.  \nA non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nSuch a line can meet the union \\(C\\cup R\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the whole column, the whole row, or at most one point of each).\n\nLet the family contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nThe sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k\\) points of the column \\(C\\) and at most \\(k\\) points of the row \\(R\\); therefore they cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(C\\cup R\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points of \\(C\\cup R\\), but this trivial bound is far larger than needed.  \nWhat matters is that **every** point of \\(C\\cup R\\) must be covered, so we need  \n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)\\ge 2n-1,\n\\]\n\nbecause each non‑sunny line contributes at least one new point of \\(C\\cup R\\) (otherwise it would be identical to a sunny line, contradicting distinctness).  \nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1-\\frac{2n-1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nThere exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPut  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\ge 2).\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non‑sunny.\n\n**(ii) Sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;x-y=j .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(+1\\); consequently \\(S_j\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions and is sunny.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\cup\\{S_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct: the vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts, while the sunny lines have distinct constants on the right‑hand side of (2); a vertical line cannot coincide with a line of slope \\(+1\\).\n\nWe now verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le m\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\) and lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>m\\).*  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a-b \\le a-1\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, using \\(a>m=n-k\\) and the hypothesis \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na-b\\le a-1\\le n-1\\le k+(n-2k)\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThe last inequality follows from \\(k\\le n/2\\), which gives \\(n-1\\le k+(n-2k)\\) and therefore \\(a-b\\le k\\).  \nCombining with the lower bound in (4) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a-b\\le k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x-y=a-b\\) and belongs to the sunny line \\(S_{a-b}\\) defined in (2).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 2 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 3 proves that no configuration can contain more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
