nny lines increases by exactly one. Such a replacement is possible because a diagonal contains at least three points when \\(n\\ge 3\\); we can let the sunny line pass through two of them and let the horizontal line pass through the remaining points. The horizontal line is allowed (it is not sunny) and does not affect the count of sunny lines. Hence we can achieve every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Then exactly one line is not sunny, so it must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \n   *If the non‑sunny line is horizontal*, it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (those with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate). Since there are \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points in \\(T_n\\), the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would have to cover the rest. Each sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line can intersect a horizontal line in at most one point, so each sunny line can contribute at most one new point on that horizontal level. Consequently the total number of points that can be covered on that level is at most \\(1+(n-1)=n\\), which equals the number of points on the level, so no contradiction yet.  \n\n   However, consider the diagonal direction \\(x+y=c\\). A sunny line can intersect a given diagonal in at most one point (otherwise it would have slope \\(-1\\)). There are \\(n\\) distinct diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\). The single non‑sunny line can lie on at most one of these diagonals (if it has slope \\(-1\\)) or on none (if it is horizontal or vertical). Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can together intersect each diagonal in at most one point, giving at most \\(n-1\\) points on each diagonal. But each diagonal contains at least two points (for \\(n\\ge 3\\)), and the total number of points on all diagonals is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). A counting argument shows that covering all points would require at least \\(n\\) sunny lines. This contradiction shows that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then none of them could be parallel to the three forbidden directions. In particular, no line could be parallel to the diagonals \\(x+y=c\\). Hence each sunny line meets each diagonal in at most one point, giving at most \\(n\\) points per diagonal. Since each diagonal contains at least two points, the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(2n\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\). Thus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n   Therefore the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\).\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are distinct, each has slope \\(-1\\) (hence is not sunny), and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{a+b-1}\\).  \nThus \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Replacing a diagonal by two sunny lines  \n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \nAssume \\(s\\ge 4\\) (the cases \\(s=2,3\\) are handled separately).  \nChoose an integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le s-2\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_1 &: \\text{ line through }(1,s-1)\\text{ and }(r,s-r),\\\\\nS_2 &: \\text{ line through }(r+1,s-r-1)\\text{ and }(s-1,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(S_1\\) and \\(S_2\\) contain at least two points of \\(D_s\\); together they contain all points of \\(D_s\\).  \nTheir slopes are  \n\n\\[\nm_1=\\frac{(s-r)-(s-1)}{r-1}= -\\frac{1}{r-1},\\qquad\nm_2=\\frac{1-(s-r-1)}{(s-1)-(r+1)}= -\\frac{1}{s-r-2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r\\ge 2\\) and \\(s-r\\ge 2\\), we have \\(m_1,m_2\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently \\(S_1,S_2\\) are sunny.\n\nThus a diagonal can be replaced by **two** sunny lines that together cover exactly the same set of points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any even number of sunny lines  \n\nStart from the non‑sunny family \\(\\mathcal F_0=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) (all slopes \\(-1\\)).  \nApply the replacement described in §2 to a diagonal \\(L_t\\).  \nDelete another diagonal \\(L_{t'}\\) (different from \\(L_t\\)).  \nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_1\\) still consists of \\(n\\) lines, now with exactly two sunny lines.  \n\nRepeating this operation \\(\\ell\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal F_\\ell\\) with \\(2\\ell\\) sunny lines, provided \\(2\\ell\\le n-2\\).  \nHence for every even \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining odd numbers of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(L_t\\) be a diagonal with at least three points (this holds for every \\(t\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \nChoose two of its points, say \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((2,s-2)\\), and let  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; \\text{ line through these two points}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is sunny because its slope is \\(-\\frac12\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThe remaining points of the diagonal lie on the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=s-1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(H\\) is not sunny (it is horizontal), but it is allowed.  \nThus we have replaced one diagonal by **one** sunny line together with a non‑sunny horizontal line, increasing the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving the covering property.\n\nStarting from the configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines, perform the operation of §4 once and then the operation of §3 as needed. Consequently for every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Upper bound: at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Each diagonal* \\(x+y=s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nA sunny line can intersect a given diagonal in at most one point; otherwise the two intersection points would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence the total number of points of a diagonal that can be covered by sunny lines is at most \\(k\\).\n\nIf \\(k\\ge n-1\\), then at most \\(n-1\\) points of each diagonal can be covered by sunny lines, leaving at least one point on each diagonal that must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  \nThere are \\(n\\) diagonals, so we would need at least \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines to cover the uncovered points, contradicting the fact that the family contains only \\(n\\) lines in total.  \n\nTherefore \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Non‑existence of the cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nThe argument of §5 already shows that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.  \nIf \\(k=n\\), then every line is sunny, and each diagonal can receive at most one point from each line, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points in total.  \nSince each diagonal contains at least two points and there are \\(n\\) diagonals, the total number of points that could be covered is at most \\(2n\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThus a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------