"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Covering set.**  \n   The required points are  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Zero sunny lines.**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(L_t:\\;x+y=t+1\\;(1\\le t\\le n)\\) all have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are not sunny) and each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them. Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n3. **Replacing a diagonal by two sunny lines.**  \n   For a fixed diagonal \\(D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\) choose an integer \\(r\\) with \\(2\\le r\\le s-2\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   S_1 &: \\text{ line through }(1,s-1)\\text{ and }(r,s-r),\\\\\n   S_2 &: \\text{ line through }(r+1,s-r-1)\\text{ and }(s-1,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Both lines contain at least two points of \\(D_s\\) and together contain all points of \\(D_s\\). Their slopes are  \n\n   \\[\n   m_1=-\\frac1{r-1},\\qquad m_2=-\\frac1{s-r-2},\n   \\]\n\n   which are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_1,S_2\\) are sunny.  \n   Consequently a diagonal can be replaced by **two** sunny lines that cover exactly the same set of points.\n\n4. **Obtaining any even \\(k\\).**  \n   Start from the non\u2011sunny family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).  \n   Pick a diagonal \\(L_t\\) (i.e. the line \\(x+y=t+1\\)) with \\(t\\ge 3\\) and replace it by the two sunny lines \\(S_1,S_2\\) of step\u202f3.  \n   To keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\), delete another diagonal line \\(L_{t'}\\) (any diagonal distinct from the one just split).  \n   The new family still has \\(n\\) lines, now with exactly two sunny lines.  \n   Repeating this operation \\(\\ell\\) times yields a configuration with \\(2\\ell\\) sunny lines, provided \\(2\\ell\\le n-2\\).  \n   Hence for every even \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration exists.\n\n5. **Obtaining odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(L_t\\) be a diagonal containing at least three points (this holds for every \\(t\\) when \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \n   Choose the two extreme points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t,1)\\) of this diagonal and the intermediate point \\((2,t-1)\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\; \\text{ line through }(1,t)\\text{ and }(2,t-1),\\qquad\n   H:\\; \\text{ horizontal line }y=t .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S\\) is \\(-\\frac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(S\\) is sunny.  \n   The horizontal line \\(H\\) is not sunny (it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) but is allowed.  \n   The three points of the diagonal are covered by \\(S\\cup H\\); all other points of the diagonal lie on \\(H\\).  \n   Hence we have replaced one diagonal by **one** sunny line together with a non\u2011sunny horizontal line, increasing the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving the covering property.\n\n   Starting from the configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines, perform the operation of step\u202f5 once and then apply the even\u2011\\(k\\) construction of step\u202f4 as needed. Consequently every odd integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) is also attainable.\n\n6. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Consider any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   *Each diagonal* \\(D_s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   A sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of the same diagonal, for otherwise the line would have slope \\(-1\\). Hence a sunny line meets a given diagonal in **at most one** point.  \n\n   Therefore the total number of points of a diagonal that can be supplied by sunny lines is at most \\(k\\).  \n   If \\(k\\ge n-1\\), then on each of the \\(n\\) diagonals at least one point would have to be supplied by a non\u2011sunny line.  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can be parallel to at most one diagonal (if its slope is \\(-1\\)) or to none (if it is horizontal or vertical). Consequently at least \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines would be required, contradicting the fact that the whole family consists of only \\(n\\) lines.  \n\n   Hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n7. **Non\u2011existence of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   The argument of step\u202f6 already excludes \\(k=n-1\\).  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each diagonal could receive at most one point from each line, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points in total. Since every diagonal contains at least two points, the total number of points that could be covered would be at most \\(2n\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Thus \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nCombining steps\u202f4\u20137 we obtain that the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
