\) (so \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le v\\).  \nThen the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>v\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(v+h+d=n\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(v+1)=h+d .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b\\le h\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\) contains the point.\n\nIf \\(b>h\\) then \\(b\\ge h+1\\); consequently\n\n\\[\na+b\\ge (v+1)+(h+1)=v+h+2\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(d+1\\), and the diagonal line\n\\(x+y=a+b\\in\\mathcal D\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\nHence we have a covering of \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\nWe now show how to turn a chosen non‑sunny line into a sunny one without destroying the covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2 (Sunny replacement).**  \nLet \\(L\\) be a line belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) that contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any finite set of distinct lines (already chosen) that does **not** contain a line parallel to \\(L\\).  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) passing through the same two points of \\(T_n\\) and distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe treat the three possible directions separately.\n\n*Vertical case.*  \nSuppose \\(L\\) is \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,v\\}\\) and contains the two points\n\\((c,b_1)\\) and \\((c,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\).  \nConsider the line through \\((c,b_1)\\) and \\((c+1,b_2)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{b_2-b_1}{1}\\neq0\\) and, because the denominator is \\(1\\), the slope cannot be \\(-1\\).  \nHence the line is sunny.  \nIts equation is \\(y-(b_1)= (b_2-b_1)(x-c)\\); the intercept is \\(b_1-(b_2-b_1)c\\), which is different from the intercept of any line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) because none of those lines is vertical.  \nThus the new line is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and passes through the two required points.\n\n*Horizontal case.*  \nIf \\(L\\) is \\(y=c\\) with points \\((a_1,c)\\) and \\((a_2,c)\\) (\\(a_1\\neq a_2\\)), take the line through \\((a_1,c)\\) and \\((a_2,c+1)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{a_2-a_1}\\neq0\\) and cannot be \\(-1\\); therefore it is sunny and, as before, distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Diagonal case.*  \nIf \\(L\\) is \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,d+1\\}\\)) and contains \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) (hence also \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\)), consider the line through \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2+1,b_2)\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{(a_2+1)-a_1}\n   =\\frac{-(a_2-a_1)}{(a_2-a_1)+1}\n   =-\\,\\frac{a_2-a_1}{a_2-a_1+1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(-1\\) (the denominator exceeds the numerator) and also different from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\).  \nHence the line is sunny and, because its intercept differs from that of any line in \\(\\mathcal L\\), it is new.\n\nIn each of the three cases we have produced a sunny line \\(L'\\) that contains the same two points of \\(T_n\\) as \\(L\\) and that does not coincide with any line already present. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nStart with the covering \\(\\mathcal L_0=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) from Claim 1; it consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nWe shall replace, one by one, exactly \\(k\\) of these lines by sunny ones, using Lemma 2.\n\nAssume that after \\(t\\) steps (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) we have a set \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause the original families each contain at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (unless the corresponding parameter \\(v,h\\) or \\(d\\) equals \\(0\\); in that case we simply choose a different non‑sunny line that does contain two points), there exists a non‑sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L_t\\) that contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nApply Lemma 2 with \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal L_t\\) to obtain a sunny line \\(L'\\) passing through the same two points of \\(T_n\\) and distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L_t\\).  \nReplace \\(L\\) by \\(L'\\) and set \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}=(\\mathcal L_t\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L'\\}\\).\n\nThe two points formerly covered by \\(L\\) are now covered by \\(L'\\); all other points remain covered because the other lines are unchanged.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\), contains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines, and consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nRepeating this procedure \\(k\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Non‑existence of other values\n\nFrom Section 1 we already have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction of Section 4 shows that every integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \nConsequently no other non‑negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
