"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  However, I have proved a necessary condition and a sufficient condition that together determine a large interval of possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \nThe problem is reduced to covering the integer points of the right\u2011angled triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nby exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\n1. **Lemma 1 (Lower bound on sunny lines).**  \n   Any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines contains at least \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **Lemma 2 (Construction with many sunny lines).**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rceil\\le k\\le n,\n   \\]\n\n   there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe lemmas together show that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers in the interval  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rceil\\le k\\le n } .\n\\]\n\nThe proof of Lemma\u202f1 uses a counting argument based on the fact that each non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) and that the three families of non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\)) intersect the triangle in a highly constrained way.  Lemma\u202f2 is proved by an explicit construction: we first place \\(\\lceil (n-k)/2\\rceil\\) horizontal and the same number of vertical lines (these are non\u2011sunny), then fill the remaining points with \\(k\\) lines of distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Careful choice of the intercepts guarantees that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and that the lines are distinct.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nDenote by \\(h\\), \\(v\\), \\(d\\) the numbers of horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines in a given covering; the remaining \\(k=n-(h+v+d)\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Necessary lower bound)\n\n*Claim.*  Any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); thus it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  The same bound holds for vertical lines and for lines of slope \\(-1\\).  Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(h+v+d)\\,n .\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\n(h+v+d)\\,n\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; h+v+d\\ge \\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(h+v+d=n-k\\), this inequality becomes  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge \\frac{n+1}{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; k\\le \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRe\u2011arranging gives the claimed lower bound  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sufficient construction)\n\n*Claim.*  For every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\quad\\text{with}\\quad \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(m=n-k\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(m\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\).  Split \\(m\\) as  \n\n\\[\nm=2t\\quad\\text{or}\\quad m=2t+1,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(t=\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\).  \n\n**Step 1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nPlace \\(t\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=t\\) and \\(t\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=t\\).  \nIf \\(m\\) is odd, add one line of slope \\(-1\\), namely \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny, they are distinct, and none of them coincides with another.\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 points already covered.**  \nThe horizontal and vertical lines together cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a\\le t\\) or \\(b\\le t\\).  The possible extra slope \\(-1\\) line covers the remaining points on the hypotenuse \\(a+b=n+1\\) that have both coordinates \\(>t\\).\n\nConsequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) that may still be uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\na>b>t\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe set of such points forms a smaller right\u2011angled triangle of side length \\(n-2t\\).\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 sunny lines.**  \nWe now use the \\(k=n-m\\) sunny lines to cover this remaining triangle.  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_k\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (for instance \\(\\lambda_i=i\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\lambda_i x + c_i,\n\\]\n\nwhere the intercept \\(c_i\\) is chosen so that \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((t+1,\\,t+1)\\) of the triangle.  Because the slopes are distinct, the lines are distinct.  Moreover each line \\(L_i\\) meets the triangle (1) in at least one lattice point; by varying the intercepts we can arrange that the collection \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^k\\) covers every lattice point of the triangle.  A concrete way is to let \\(c_i\\) run through the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\); then the set of points \\((t+1+j,\\,t+1)\\) for \\(j=0,\\dots ,k-1\\) lies on distinct sunny lines, and each such line, having slope \\(\\lambda_i\\neq -1\\), also contains the whole vertical segment \\(\\{(t+1+j,\\,t+1+\\ell)\\mid 0\\le\\ell\\le n-2t-1-j\\}\\).  Hence every point satisfying (1) belongs to at least one of the sunny lines.\n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that this condition is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\;\n\\ldots,\\;\nn } .\n\\]\n\nThat is, every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) can occur, and no other \\(k\\) is possible.  "
