l\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sufficiency)\n\n**Claim.** For every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nPut \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).  \nBecause of Lemma 1 we have \\(m\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\); in particular \\(m\\ge 2\\).  \n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nm=2t\\quad\\text{or}\\quad m=2t+1,\n\\qquad t=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Step 1 – non‑sunny lines  \n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nTake the \\(t\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=t .\n\\]\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nTake the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=t .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\) is odd we add one line of slope \\(-1\\), namely  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non‑sunny, they are distinct, and their total number is exactly \\(m\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2 – points already covered  \n\nThe horizontal and vertical lines together cover every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) whose\n\\(x\\le t\\) **or** \\(y\\le t\\).  \nIf the extra slope \\(-1\\) line is present, it additionally covers the points of the hypotenuse\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) with both coordinates \\(>t\\).\n\nHence the only points that may remain uncovered are those satisfying  \n\n\\[\nt < a,\\; t < b,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese points form a right‑angled triangle of side length \\(n-2t\\); denote this set by \\(T'\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3 – sunny lines covering \\(T'\\)\n\nWe have to cover \\(T'\\) with the remaining \\(k=n-m\\) lines, all of which must be sunny.\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for concreteness let  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_i = i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\lambda_i x + c_i ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the intercept \\(c_i\\) is chosen so that \\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice point\n\\((t+1,\\,t+1)\\in T'\\).  Thus \\(c_i = t+1-\\lambda_i (t+1)\\).\n\nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct.  \nEach line \\(L_i\\) contains the whole vertical segment  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t+1+j,\\;t+1+\\lambda_i j)\\mid j=0,1,\\dots ,\\,n-2t-1-j\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich lies inside \\(T'\\) (the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to\n\\(j+\\lambda_i j\\le n-2t\\) and holds for all admissible \\(j\\) because \\(\\lambda_i\\ge 1\\)).  \nConsequently every lattice point of \\(T'\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) form a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nLemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nsuch a configuration exists.  \nTherefore the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor \\}\\; } .\n\\]"
