overed only by the removed line \\(x=c\\) is also covered by the newly inserted sunny line \\(S_{c}\\).  \n   Thus the total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\) (we delete one line and add one line), and the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one.\n\n4. **Induction on \\(k\\).**  \n   Begin with \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) (0 sunny lines).  \n   Apply the safe replacement described in step\u202f3 successively for \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   After the \\(k\\)-th step we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal C_{k}\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n   * covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\);\n   * contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\)).\n\n5. **Upper bound.**  \n   Obviously at most \\(n\\) sunny lines can appear when only \\(n\\) lines are used.\n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{0}= \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical lines}}\n                 \\;\\cup\\;\n                 \\underbrace{\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}}_{\\text{diagonal line}} .\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) are non\u2011sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal C_{0}|=n\\).\n\n*Claim.*  Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal C_{0}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}