amily; hence it stays covered.\n* Every other point of \\(T_{n}\\) either does not involve the abscissa \\(c\\) (hence was never on the deleted line) or, if it had abscissa \\(c\\), it is now covered by \\(S_{c}\\) because all points with abscissa \\(c\\) and ordinate \\(b\\ge 1\\) satisfy \\(b\\le n-c\\) and therefore lie on the segment of \\(S_{c}\\) between \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+1,n-c)\\).\n\nThus the family after the operation still covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nThe total number of lines is unchanged (one line removed, one line added), and the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one (the newly added line \\(S_{c}\\) is sunny, while the deleted line \\(x=c\\) was non\u2011sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\nStart from the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\mathcal C_{0}\\cup\\{y=1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(n+1\\) lines and \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) perform the safe replacement described in \u00a7\u202f3 with \\(c=i\\).  \nAfter the \\(i\\)-th step the family \\(\\mathcal F_{i}\\) has exactly \\(n\\) lines (the extra line \\(y=1\\) remains throughout) and exactly \\(i\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{i}\\)).  \n\nConsequently, for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nthe family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\), and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Upper bound  \n\nSince only \\(n\\) lines are used, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nAll non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
