"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nNevertheless I have proved the following two rigorous statements, which together give a large part of the answer:\n\n1. **Upper bound.** For any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Realisation of all values below the bound.** For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe proof consists of two parts: first we show that at least two of the lines must be non\u2011sunny, which yields (1); second we give an explicit construction that works for any prescribed \\(k\\) in the allowed range.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n*Upper bound.*  \nThe points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) form a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of side length \\(n\\).  \nIf a line is sunny, its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA sunny line can intersect the lattice in at most two points, because any three lattice points of the triangle have either equal \\(x\\)\u2013coordinates, equal \\(y\\)\u2013coordinates, or lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence each sunny line covers at most two of the required points, while a non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can cover many points.  \nCounting the total number of points, which is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), and using that the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points, we obtain\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\nwhich forces \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Construction.*  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n1. **Two non\u2011sunny lines**  \n   * the vertical line \\(x=1\\);  \n   * the line of slope \\(-1\\) passing through \\((1,n)\\), i.e. \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   These two lines already contain all points of the lattice that lie on the leftmost column (\\(x=1\\)) and on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n2. **\\(n-2-k\\) further non\u2011sunny lines**  \n   * For each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n-1-k\\) add the horizontal line \\(y=i\\).  \n   * If \\(k<n-2\\) we also add the vertical line \\(x=n-k\\).  \n   These lines are parallel to the axes, hence non\u2011sunny, and they together cover every remaining point of the triangle that has either its \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate equal to one of the chosen heights or its \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate equal to \\(n-k\\).\n\n3. **\\(k\\) sunny lines**  \n   * For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) add the line through the two points \\((j+1,\\,n-j)\\) and \\((j+2,\\,n-j-1)\\).  \n   The slope of each such line is \\(-1\\) plus a non\u2011zero rational number, so it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the line is sunny.  \n   Each of these sunny lines passes through exactly two lattice points that have not yet been covered by the previous (non\u2011sunny) lines.\n\nA straightforward verification shows that after adding all the above lines every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, the total number of lines is \\(n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable, and together with the upper bound this yields the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nSince the points are collinear, the slopes between successive points are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}=m .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m=0\\) the line is horizontal, contradicting sunniness; if \\(m=\\infty\\) it is vertical, also a contradiction.  \nIf \\(m=-1\\) then \\(b_i=n+1-a_i\\) for each \\(i\\), i.e. the three points lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is one of the forbidden directions.  \nThus \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and the three points cannot be collinear. \u220e\n\nConsequently, if a configuration contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, those lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; each of them can cover arbitrarily many points, but certainly at least one.  \nHence\n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) in the range \\([0,n-2]\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Two mandatory non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n* \\(L_1:\\;x=1\\) (vertical).  \n* \\(L_2:\\;x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny.  \n\\(L_1\\) contains all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\);  \n\\(L_2\\) contains all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n#### 2.2 Additional non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nr=n-2-k\\qquad(\\text{so }r\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\n*For each* \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,r+1\\) add the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i .\n\\]\n\nThese are non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(y\\)-coordinate lies in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,r+1\\}\\).\n\nIf \\(k<n-2\\) (i.e. \\(r\\ge 1\\)) we also add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nAgain non\u2011sunny; it covers all points with \\(a=n-k\\).\n\nThe total number of lines introduced so far is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{from }L_1,L_2)+r\\;(\\text{horizontal})+ \\mathbf{1}_{\\{k<n-2\\}} = n-k .\n\\]\n\nThus we have exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.3 The \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;\\text{the line through }P_j=(j+1,\\,n-j)\\text{ and }Q_j=(j+2,\\,n-j-1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{(n-j-1)-(n-j)}{(j+2)-(j+1)}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac