            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line can contain at most two points of the set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   Proof: three collinear points of \\(T_n\\) would have to be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), which are precisely the forbidden directions.  \n   Hence \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) points, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) (non‑sunny) lines cover at least one point each.  \n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), we obtain  \n   \\[\n   2k+(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Realisation of every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   For a fixed \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) we construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   - \\(L_1:\\;x=1\\) (vertical).  \n   - \\(L_2:\\;x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   - For each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n-1-k\\) add the horizontal line \\(H_i:\\;y=i\\).  \n   - If \\(k<n-2\\) add the vertical line \\(V:\\;x=n-k\\).  \n\n   These are \\(n-k\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny. One checks that every point of \\(T_n\\) whose\n   \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(1\\) or whose \\(x+y=n+1\\) or whose \\(y\\)-coordinate belongs to\n   \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-1-k\\}\\) (or whose \\(x=n-k\\) when this line is present) is already covered.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n   \\[\n   S_j:\\text{ the line through }P_j=(j+1,\\;n-j)\\text{ and }Q_j=(j+2,\\;n-j-1).\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(n-j-1)-(n-j)}{(j+2)-(j+1)}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{-1}{1}=-1+\\frac{