"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist configurations of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Geometry of the required point set.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the set of integer points inside the right\u2011angled isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \n   It contains \\(\\displaystyle |T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}\\) points.\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny directions.**  \n   A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff its slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Hence the three families  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}\n   \\]\n   consist entirely of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n3. **Construction with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the two side\u2011lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) together with the diagonal lines\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n-1 .\n   \\]\n   These are \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\):\n   * the side\u2011lines cover the two legs of the triangle,\n   * each diagonal \\(x+y=c\\;(2\\le c\\le n-1)\\) covers the points with \\(a+b=c\\).\n\n4. **Construction with \\(k=1\\).**  \n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 choose a point that is not on any of the three forbidden families.*  \n     Let  \n     \\[\n     P=\\bigl(2,\\,n-1\\bigr).\n     \\]\n     Since \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(2\\ge 1\\) and \\(n-1\\ge 1\\); moreover  \n     \\[\n     P\\notin\\{x=1\\},\\qquad P\\notin\\{y=1\\},\\qquad \n     P\\notin\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\},\n     \\]\n     because \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\) and the diagonal lines we shall use will be\n     \\[\n     x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n-2 .\n     \\]\n     Thus the sum \\(a+b=n+1\\) of the coordinates of \\(P\\) does **not** appear among the chosen diagonal constants.\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 cover all points except \\(P\\) with non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n     Take the following \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n     \\[\n     L_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \n     L_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n     L_{2+i}:\\;x+y=i+1\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-3).\n     \\]\n     The diagonal constants run from \\(2\\) up to \\(n-2\\); consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates sum to a value \\(\\le n-2\\) lies on one of these diagonals, while the two side\u2011lines cover the points with first or second coordinate equal to \\(1\\).  \n     The only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are those with\n     \\[\n     a+b=n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a+b=n+1 .\n     \\]\n     Among them the point \\(P=(2,n-1)\\) (which satisfies \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\)) is uncovered; all other points with sum \\(n\\) or \\(n+1\\) are either on the side\u2011lines (if one coordinate equals \\(1\\)) or on one of the already used diagonals (if the sum is \\(\\le n-2\\)). Hence after Step\u202f2 the only uncovered point of \\(T_n\\) is \\(P\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 add a single sunny line through \\(P\\).*  \n     Choose any line through \\(P\\) whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); for definiteness take\n     \\[\n     \\ell:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{n}{2}+1 .\n     \\]\n     The slope \\(-\\frac12\\) is not forbidden, so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n     By construction \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(P\\) and does not coincide with any of the previously selected lines (its slope is different from those of the side\u2011lines and of the diagonals). Consequently the family\n     \\[\n     \\{L_1,L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n-1},\\ell\\}\n     \\]\n     consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and together they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with at least two sunny lines existed. Remove all sunny lines; the remaining at most \\(n-2\\) lines are all non\u2011sunny, i.e. belong to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\).  \n   Any line from \\(\\mathcal H\\) can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (all points with a given second coordinate), the same bound holds for a line from \\(\\mathcal V\\), and a line from \\(\\mathcal D\\) with constant \\(c\\) covers exactly \\(c-1\\) points.  \n   The total number of points to be covered is \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}\\). A straightforward counting argument shows that with at most \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines one cannot reach this number:\n   \\[\n   \\max\\bigl\\{(n-2)\\cdot n,\\;(n-2)\\cdot (n-1),\\;(n-2)\\cdot (n-2)\\bigr\\}\n   = (n-2)n < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|\n   \\quad\\text{for }n\\ge 3 .\n   \\]\n   Hence at least \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines are necessary, which leaves room for at most one sunny line. Therefore \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) with the impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \nL_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad \nL_{2+i}:\\;x+y=i+1\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nAll of them belong to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\), hence none is sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nThe line \\(x=1\\) contains all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); the line \\(y=1\\) contains all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on the two sides.  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and we have exhibited a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\n#### 2.1 Choice of a point not lying on any non\u2011sunny line we shall use\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP=(2,\\,n-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), both coordinates of \\(P\\) are at least \\(1\\) and \\(P\\in T_n\\) because\n\\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\).\n\nWe shall employ the following \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_{2+i} &: x+y=i+1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal constants occurring in (1) are \\(2,3,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \nConsequently the sum of the coordinates of any point lying on one of the diagonals in (1) is at most \\(n-2\\).  \nSince the sum for \\(P\\) equals \\(n+1\\), we have \\(P\\notin L_j\\) for every \\(j\\) in (1).\n\n#### 2.2 Coverage of all points except \\(P\\)\n\n*Side\u2011lines.*  \n\\(L_1\\) covers all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);  \n\\(L_2\\) covers all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).\n\n*Diagonals.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) the line \\(x+y=c\\) (present in (1)) contains exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinate sum is \\(\\le n-2\\) is covered by a diagonal, and every point whose sum is \\(=n\\) or \\(=n+1\\) is covered by a side\u2011line **except** the point \\(P\\) (which has sum \\(n+1\\) and does not lie on either side).  \nTherefore after the \\(n-1\\) lines in (1) the only uncovered point of \\(T_n\\) is \\(P\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Adding a single sunny line\n\nTake the line\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{n}{2}+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); thus \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nSubstituting the coordinates of \\(P\\) into (2) gives\n\\[\nn-1 = -\\frac12\\cdot 2 + \\frac{n}{2}+1,\n\\]\nso \\(P\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), \\(\\ell\\) coincides with none of the lines in (1); consequently the family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n-1},\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (namely \\(\\ell\\)) is sunny, and together they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus a configuration with precisely one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration can have two or more sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least two sunny lines.  \nRemove all sunny lines; at most \\(n-2\\) lines remain, and each of them belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\).\n\n*Maximum number of points a non\u2011sunny line can cover.*  \n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (all points with second coordinate \\(c\\));\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) also contains at most \\(n\\) points;\n* a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n-1\\) points.\n\nHence any collection of at most \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most\n\\[\n(n-2)\\cdot n = n^2-2n\n\\]\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\nBut \\(|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have\n\\[\nn^2-2n < \\frac{n^2+n}{2},\n\\]\nso the points of \\(T_n\\) cannot be completely covered by the non\u2011sunny lines alone.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines are necessary, which leaves room for **at most one** sunny line.  \nTherefore a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n- Configurations with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exist (Section\u202f1).  \n- Configurations with \\(k=1\\) sunny line exist (Section\u202f2).  \n- No configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines (Section\u202f3).\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0\\text{ or }k=1.}\n\\]"
