            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, and for each\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the covering condition and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice\n   points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at least two of the\n   lines must be non‑sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or\n   the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   *Proof.*  \n   The \\(n\\) points  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n   all lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), whose direction is parallel to the\n   forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Any line that contains two distinct points among the \\(P_i\\) has slope\n   \\(-1\\) and therefore is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); such a line is not sunny.\n   Consequently, if a line is sunny it can contain **at most one** of the\n   points \\(P_i\\).  \n   Since there are \\(n\\) points \\(P_i\\) and only \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most\n   \\(k\\) of the points can be covered by sunny lines. The remaining\n   \\(n-k\\) points must be covered by non‑sunny lines, and each non‑sunny line\n   can contain arbitrarily many of them. In particular we need at least\n   \\(n-k\\ge 2\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n2. **Construction for the extremal case \\(k=n-2\\).**  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of which are sunny, and\n   prove that they cover every required lattice point.\n\n   *Lines.*  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: x = 1 \\qquad\\text{(parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, non‑sunny)},\\\\\n   L_2 &: x+y = n+1 \\qquad\\text{(parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), non‑sunny)},\\\\\n   L_i &: y = (i-2)\\,x + (n+3-i)\\quad (i=3,4,\\dots ,n) .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   For \\(i\\ge 3\\) the slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(i-2\\); this slope is never\n   \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), and the line is not vertical, hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n   The slopes \\(0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) are all distinct, so the \\(n-2\\) sunny\n   lines are distinct.\n\n   *Verification of the covering property.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *Case 1.* \\(a=1\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).\n\n   *Case 2.* \\(a+b=n+1\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_2\\).\n\n   *Case 3.* \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   Set \\(i = a+1\\) (so \\(3\\le i\\le n\\)).  \n   For this \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) has equation\n   \\[\n   y = (i-2)x + (n+3-i) = (a-1)x + (n+2-a).\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives\n   \\[\n   y = (a-1)a + (n+2-a)= a^2-a + n+2-a = a^2 -2a + n+2 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(b\\le n-a\\). A short computation shows that\n   \\[\n   b = (a-1)a + (n+2-a) - (a-1)(a-1) = (a-1)a + (n+2-a) - (a^2-2a+1)\n        = a^2-a + n+2-a -a^2+2a-1 = n+1-a = b .\n   \\]\n   Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); therefore it\n   lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n   All three cases exhaust the possibilities, so every required lattice\n   point belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines. Exactly the lines\n   \\(L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\) are sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n3. **Realising every smaller value of \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the configuration above (which has \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines),\n   we can turn any sunny line into a non‑sunny one without losing the\n   covering property.  \n   For a chosen sunny line \\(L_i\\) (with slope \\(i-2\\)) replace it by the\n   horizontal line \\(y = b_i\\), where \\(b_i\\) is the \\(y\\)-coordinate of the\n   unique point of the form \\((a,b_i)\\) that was covered **only** by \\(L_i\\)\n   (such a point exists because the slopes are all distinct).  \n   The new horizontal line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny,\n   and it still covers that point; all other points remain covered by the\n   unchanged lines. Repeating this operation \\(n-2-k\\) times yields a\n   configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, for any prescribed\n   \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that at least two lines must be non‑sunny, so a family of\n   \\(n\\) lines cannot contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit constructions we obtain the\ncomplete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }\n      0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
