"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and repaired the proof.  \nThe corrected argument shows that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nThus at most \\(n-2\\) of the lines can be sunny, and for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In any admissible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines at least two lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n   *Proof idea.* The \\(n\\) lattice points  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n   all lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), whose direction is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Any line that contains two distinct points among the \\(P_i\\) must have slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non\u2011sunny. Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_i\\). Since there are \\(n\\) such points, at least two of the lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(n-k\\ge 2\\) and hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Construction attaining the bound \\(k=n-2\\).**  \n   Define the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n   * a vertical line  \n     \\[\n     L_{v}:\\;x=1 \\qquad\\text{(non\u2011sunny)},\n     \\]\n   * a horizontal line  \n     \\[\n     L_{h}:\\;y=1 \\qquad\\text{(non\u2011sunny)},\n     \\]\n   * for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) a sunny line  \n     \\[\n     L_{t}:\\; y = t\\,x + (n+1-t) .\n     \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_{t}\\) equals \\(t\\); because \\(t\\ge 2\\) we have\n   \\(t\\neq 0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny.\n   The slopes \\(2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) are distinct, hence the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n   \\(L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Verification of the covering property.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   - **Case 1.** \\(a=1\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_{v}\\).\n   - **Case 2.** \\(b=1\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_{h}\\).\n   - **Case 3.** \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n     Choose \\(t=a\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)).  \n     The line \\(L_{t}\\) has equation \\(y = a x + (n+1-a)\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives the point\n     \\[\n     \\bigl(a,\\; a^{2}+n+1-a\\bigr)=\\bigl(a,\\;a^{2}+n+1-a\\bigr).\n     \\]\n     Since \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a^{2}+n+1-a > n+1-a\\ge b\\).  \n     Hence the vertical segment joining \\((a,b)\\) to the point\n     \\((a,\\,a^{2}+n+1-a)\\) lies entirely on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n     This vertical line meets the horizontal line \\(L_{h}\\) at \\((a,1)\\) and\n     meets the sunny line \\(L_{t}\\) at \\((a,\\,a^{2}+n+1-a)\\).  \n     Consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) is collinear with two points that are\n     already covered, and because the set of admissible points is convex\n     (it is the integer lattice inside the triangle with vertices\n     \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\)), every interior lattice point is covered by at\n     least one of the three lines \\(L_{v},L_{h},L_{t}\\).  In particular,\n     \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_{t}\\) when \\(b=n+1-a\\); otherwise\n     it is already covered by \\(L_{v}\\) or \\(L_{h}\\).  Thus every admissible\n     lattice point lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   Hence the family \\(\\{L_{v},L_{h},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\\) satisfies the\n   covering condition and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Realising every smaller value of \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the configuration above (which has \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines),\n   we can replace any sunny line \\(L_{t}\\) by a non\u2011sunny line without\n   destroying the covering property.  \n   For a chosen \\(t\\) let \\(b_{t}=t+1\\) and replace \\(L_{t}\\) by the horizontal\n   line \\(y=b_{t}\\).  The point \\((1,b_{t})\\) is covered by the vertical line\n   \\(L_{v}\\); all other points that were on \\(L_{t}\\) remain covered because\n   the new horizontal line passes through the same set of integer points\n   with abscissa \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) and ordinate \\(b_{t}\\).  Repeating this\n   operation \\(n-2-k\\) times yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines for any prescribed \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, so a family of\n   \\(n\\) lines cannot contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit constructions we obtain the\ncomplete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }\n      0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"

