\n     L_{v}:\\;x=1 \\qquad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\n     \\]\n   * a horizontal line  \n     \\[\n     L_{h}:\\;y=1 \\qquad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\n     \\]\n   * for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) a sunny line  \n     \\[\n     L_{t}:\\; y = \\frac{n-t}{t-1}\\,x + \\frac{n-2t+2}{t-1}.\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_{t}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{n-t}{t-1}\\).  \n   Because \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) we have \\(t-1\\ge1\\) and \\(n-t\\ge1\\); consequently the slope is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), and the line is not vertical. Hence each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny, and the slopes \\(\\dfrac{n-t}{t-1}\\) are distinct, so the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Verification of the covering property.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   - **Case 1.** \\(a=1\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_{v}\\).\n   - **Case 2.** \\(b=1\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_{h}\\).\n   - **Case 3.** \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n     Choose \\(t=a\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)).  \n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation (1) of \\(L_{t}\\) gives\n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{n-a}{a-1}\\,a + \\frac{n-2a+2}{a-1}\n       = \\frac{a(n-a)+n-2a+2}{a-1}\n       = \\frac{an-a^{2}+n-2a+2}{a-1}\n       = \\frac{(n+1-a)(a-1)}{a-1}=n+1-a .\n     \\]\n     Hence the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) belongs to \\(L_{a}\\).  \n     Since \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the vertical segment joining \\((a,b)\\) to\n     \\((a,n+1-a)\\) lies entirely on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n     The endpoint \\((a,n+1-a)\\) is on \\(L_{a}\\); the other endpoint\n     \\((a,1)\\) is on the horizontal line \\(L_{h}\\).  \n     Because the set of admissible points is convex (it is exactly the set of integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\)), every lattice point of the 