            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is not \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), not\n\\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and not \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations for the extreme values\n\n#### 1.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in L_b\\).\nAll lines in (2) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore none is sunny.\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n#### 1.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in S_n\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\frac{1}{i}\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1/i\\); consequently \\(\\ell_i\\) is not parallel to\nthe \\(x\\)-axis (\\(0\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(\\infty\\)) nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(-1\\)).\nSince the slopes \\(1/i\\) are distinct, the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise\ndifferent, and each of them passes through the point \\(P_i\\).\n\nFix a row \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  Solving (4) for the intersection with\n\\(y=b\\) gives a unique point \\((x,b)\\) on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) meets\nevery horizontal row exactly once, and the union \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_i\\)\ncontains, for each row \\(b\\), precisely the \\(n+1-b\\) points of \\(S_n\\) that lie\non that row.  Therefore all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered by the \\(n\\) sunny\nlines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  Thus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer in the interval \\([0,n]\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nTake the first \\(k\\) lines of the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) defined in\n(4); denote them by \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  They are sunny and, as shown\nabove, together they already cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nAdd \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines among the set \\(\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\}\\); for definiteness\nchoose  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach line in (5) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and none of them\ncoincides with any \\(\\ell_i\\) because the latter have non‑zero slopes.  The total\nnumber of lines is therefore \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\nSince the sunny lines already cover all points of \\(S_n\\), adding the horizontal\nlines does not destroy the covering property.  Consequently the family consisting\nof the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) together with the \\(n-k\\) horizontal\nlines (5) satisfies both requirements of the problem and contains exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustiveness\n\nThe problem asks for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family\nexists.  We have exhibited explicit construction