nt satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   b-i = \\frac{1}{i+1}\\bigl(a-(n-k+i)\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); hence it lies on the corresponding sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover **all** points of \\(S\\).  \n\n3. **Putting the two families together.**  \n   The union of the \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   By the arguments above, every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one of these lines.  \n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all such \\(k\\) are admissible, and no other values are possible because the number of lines is exactly \\(n\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_a\\).  \nThus all points of  \n\n\\[\nT_n^{(1)}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\le n-k\\}\n\\]\n\nare already covered.\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nT_n^{(2)}:=T_n\\setminus T_n^{(1)}\n          =\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(n-k+i,\\;i)\\in T_n^{(2)} .\n\\]\n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\;y-i = \\frac{1}{i+1}\\bigl(x-(n-k+