f \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_a\\).  \nConsequently the subset  \n\n\\[\nT_n^{(1)}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\le n-k\\}\n\\]\n\nis already covered.\n\n**Remaining points.**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=T_n\\setminus T_n^{(1)}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_k\\) is a right\u2011angled triangle of side\u2011length \\(k\\); a direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=\\sum_{t=1}^{k}t=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n**Sunny covering of the triangle.**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(n-k+i,\\;i)\\in S_k ,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma_i:\\; y-i=\\frac{1}{i+1}\\bigl(x-(n-k+i)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(\\sigma_i\\) is \\(m_i=\\dfrac1{i+1}\\).  \nBecause \\(i+1\\ge2\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq0\\); the line is not vertical, and \\(m_i\\neq-1\\).  \nThus every \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Distinctness.*  The numbers \\(m_i\\) are pairwise different, so the \\(\\sigma_i\\) are distinct.\n\n*Containment of the designated points.*  By construction \\(P_i\\in\\sigma_i\\).\n\nWe now prove that the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) together contain **all** points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma.** For any \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\) there exists a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) such that \\((a,b)\\in\\sigma_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) and set \\(i=r\\).  \nFrom the defining inequality of \\(S_k\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = k+1-r = i+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\) can be either \\(i\\) or \\(i+1\\) (it cannot be smaller than \\(1\\)).  \n\n*If \\(b=i\\)*, then \\((a,b)=P_i\\) and belongs to \\(\\sigma_i\\).\n\n*If \\(b=i+1\\)*, then \\(a=n-k+i\\). Substituting these coordinates into the equation of \\(\\sigma_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)-i = \\frac{1}{i+1}\\bigl((n-k+i)-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds, so \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(\\sigma_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on the line \\(\\sigma_i\\) with \\(i\\) determined by its abscissa, proving the lemma. \u220e\n\nBy the lemma the union \\(\\{\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The final family of lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*   \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = (n-k)+k = n\\).  \n*   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) are sunny; the \\(\\ell_i\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \n*   Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to some \\(\\ell_i\\) (if its first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\)) or to some \\(\\sigma_i\\) (if it lies in \\(S_k\\)).  \n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies both conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Exhaustiveness\n\nThe problem asks for all possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines in a configuration of **exactly** \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nBecause a configuration cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that **every** integer in this interval is indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nAll non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) occur as the number of sunny lines in a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the required lattice points.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
