*  \n\n> **Lemma 1.** Every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\).  Write \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) and set \\(i=r\\).  \nFrom the definition of \\(S_k\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = k+1-r = i+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)y = i x + n-k-i .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=n-k+i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)y = i(n-k+i)+n-k-i = (i+1)(n-k)+i^{2}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nHence any point of the column \\(x=n-k+i\\) that belongs to \\(L_i\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\ny = n-k + \\frac{i^{2}}{i+1}= n-k + i - \\frac{i}{i+1}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause the right‑hand side is not an integer unless the fractional part \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) disappears, the only integer value of \\(y\\) allowed by (10) is  \n\n\\[\ny = i .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus the unique lattice point of the column \\(x=n-k+i\\) that lies on \\(L_i\\) is \\((n-k+i,i)\\).  \nConversely, the point \\((n-k+i,i)\\) satisfies (10) and therefore belongs to \\(L_i\\).\n\nNow (9) shows that for the given column the possible ordinates are \\(1,2,\\dots ,i+1\\).  \nAll of them except \\(i\\) are **strictly** smaller than the value prescribed by (10), so they do **not** satisfy the line equation and consequently are not on any \\(L_j\\).  \nHence the only point of the column that is covered by the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) is \\((n-k+i,i)\\).\n\nSince the set of columns \\(\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) together with the points \\((n-k+i,i)\\) (for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)) is exactly the set \\(S_k\\), the lemma follows. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The complete family of lines  \n\nCombine the vertical lines (5) with the sunny lines (8):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_k\n   =\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \\(|\\mathcal{F}_k|=(n-k)+k=n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\