----------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The complete family of lines  \n\nCombine the vertical lines (5) with the sunny lines (8):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_k\n   =\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \\(|\\mathcal{F}_k|=(n-k)+k=n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny; the \\(\\ell_j\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non‑sunny.\n\n*Covering property.*  By construction the vertical lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\); Lemma 1 shows that the lines \\(L_i\\) cover the remaining points of \\(S_k\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}_k\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both requirements of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Exhaustiveness of the values of \\(k\\)\n\nBecause any admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Consequently the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
