            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Covering the points with small first coordinate  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nIntroduce the vertical (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_a\\).  \nHence the subset  \n\n\\[\nT_n^{(1)}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\le n-k\\}\n\\]\n\nis already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The remaining triangular set  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=T_n\\setminus T_n^{(1)}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_k\\) is a right‑angled lattice triangle of side‑length \\(k\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=\\sum_{t=1}^{k}t=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Sunny lines that cover \\(S_k\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + \\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}\\,(n-k).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(m_i=\\dfrac{i}{k+1}\\).  \nBecause \\(0<m_i<1\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq0\\), \\(m_i\\neq\\infty\\) and \\(m_i\\neq-1\\); thus every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Distinctness.*  The numbers \\(m_i\\) are strictly increasing, so the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 1 – every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on exactly one \\(L_i\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\na=n-k+r\\qquad\\text{with }1\\le r\\le k,\n\\]\n\nand set \\(i=r\\).  From the definition of \\(S_k\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = k+1-r = i+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a=n-k+i\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) (9) gives\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i}{k+1}(n-k+i)+\\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}(n-k)\n   = \\frac{i^2+(n-k)(k+1)}{k+1}\n   = i .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the only integer ordinate compatible with the line equation for the column \\(x=n-k+i\\) is \\(b=i\\).  \nBecause (10) forces \\(b\\le i\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), we indeed have \\(b=i\\).  Consequently the point \\((a,b)=(n-k+i,i)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\).\n\nIf a point of the same column had \\(b\\neq i\\) then (11) would be violated, so it cannot satisfy the equation of any \\(L_j\\).  Hence each column \\(x=n-k+i\\) contributes exactly the lattice point \\((n-k+i,i)\\) to the union \\(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}L_j\\), and these points are precisely the points of \\(S_k\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The complete family of lines  \n\nCombine the vertical lines (6) with the sunny lines (9):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k\n   =\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \\(|\\mathcal F_k|=(n-k)+k=n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny; the \\(\\ell_j\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non‑sunny.\n\n*Covering property.*  The vertical lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\); Lemma 1 shows that the lines \\(L_i\\) cover the remaining points of \\(S_k\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both requirements of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Exhaustiveness of the values of \\(k\\)\n\nBecause any admissible configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Consequently the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
