w\\quad qb = pa + qc .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are coprime, the congruence \\(pa \\equiv -qc \\pmod q\\) has a unique solution for \\(a\\) modulo \\(q\\).  \nConsequently the set of integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) of (2) is a **lattice** with period \\(q\\) in the \\(a\\)-direction: if \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (2) then so does \\((a+q,b+p)\\).\n\nNow consider the strip  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid 1\\le a\\le q\\}.\n\\]\n\nAmong the points of \\(S\\) at most one satisfies (2), because two distinct points of \\(S\\) would differ by a multiple of \\(q\\) in the first coordinate, contradicting the uniqueness of the solution modulo \\(q\\).  \nTherefore **in each block of \\(q\\) consecutive columns there is at most one lattice point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\).**\n\nSince the columns of \\(T_n\\) run from \\(a=1\\) up to \\(a=n\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\), we can choose a column index \\(a_0\\) with \\(1\\le a_0\\le n\\) such that the column \\(a=a_0\\) contains **no** point of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\).  \nPick any \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a_0\\); the point \\((a_0,b)\\in T_n\\) is then not on \\(\\ell\\).  \nThus **every sunny line misses at least one required lattice point**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A configuration with only non‑sunny lines exists\n\nThe three non‑sunny directions are:\n\n* horizontal lines (\\(m=0\\));\n* vertical lines (\\(m=\\infty\\));\n* lines of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently the point lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nAll lines in (3) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and there are exactly \\(n\\) of them, as required.\n\nHence a configuration satisfying the covering condition **and containing zero sunny lines** exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Exhaustiveness of the possible numbers of sunny lines  \n\nFrom part 1 we have proved that a configuration containing even a single sunny line cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k=0\\).\n\nConversely, part 2 shows that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two given conditions exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
