y \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** *A sunny line contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\) in each column \\(x=a\\) (i.e. for each fixed integer \\(a\\)).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf two distinct points \\((a,b_1)\\) and \\((a,b_2)\\) of the same column lie on the line, then \\(b_1=ma+c=b_2\\), a contradiction. ∎\n\nConsequently a sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (one per column).  \n\nA non‑sunny line can meet many points of a single column (vertical line) or a single diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)) or a whole row (horizontal line). In any case a non‑sunny line meets **at most \\(n+1\\) points** of \\(T_n\\) because the largest possible size of a column, a row or a diagonal inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is \\(n\\) (for a column or row) or \\(n+1\\) (for the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).\n\nHence the total number of incidences (counted with multiplicity) between the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and the points of \\(T_n\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n\\le s\\cdot n + t\\cdot (n+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTogether with \\(s+t=n\\) we obtain the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\ns+t=n,\\\\[2pt]\ns\\,n + t\\,(n+1)\\ge \\dfrac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nEliminating \\(t=n-s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ns\\,n + (n-s)(n+1)\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\ns\\le \\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus no admissible configuration can contain more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWrite \\(n=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) (so \\(q=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  \n\n**Base configuration (no sunny lines).**  \nTake the \\(q\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=q\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand the \\(q\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=q .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(r=1\\) add the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)).  \nAll together we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either \\(a\\le q\\) (hence the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b\\le q\\) (hence it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)). If both \\(a,b>q\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+1\\) when \\(r=1\\); in that case the point lies on the added diagonal. Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\nHence a configuration with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines exists.\n\n---\n\n**Turning non‑sunny lines into sunny ones.**  \nConsider a pair consisting of a vertical line \\(x=i\\) and a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le q\\). Their intersection point is \\((i,j)\\). Choose any slope \\(m\\) different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) and define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i,j}: y = m(x-i)+j .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\) passes through \\((i,j)\\) and, because \\(m\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), it is sunny. Moreover, \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\) also meets the vertical line \\(x=i\\) at \\((i,j)\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) at the same point; consequently we may **replace** the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=i\\) and \\(y=j\\) by the single sunny line \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\) without losing any covered point: every point that was covered by either of the two removed lines is still covered (the point \\((i,j)\\) is now on \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\), and any other point of column \\(i\\) or row \\(j\\) is still covered by the remaining vertical or horizontal lines).\n\nPerforming this replacement for distinct pairs \\((i_1,j_1),\\dots ,(i_k,j_k)\\) reduces the number of non‑sunny lines by \\(2k\\) and increases the number of sunny lines by \\(k\\). Starting from the base configuration we can repeat the operation up to \\(q=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) times, because there are exactly \\(q\\) vertical and \\(q\\) horizontal lines available.\n\nThus for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,q\\}=\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nwe obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe upper bound (3) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, and the explicit construction above shows that every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]"
