 n + v,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(v\\) is the number of vertical lines among the \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Because the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   s+v\\le n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (1) and eliminating \\(v\\) from (3)–(4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le s n + (n-s) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; s\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(s\\) is integral,\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).**  \n   Write \\(n=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) (so \\(q=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  \n\n   *Base family (no sunny lines).*  \n   - Vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=q\\);  \n   - Horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=q\\);  \n   - If \\(r=1\\) add the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   This gives exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le q\\) (hence lies on \\(x=a\\)), or \\(b\\le q\\) (lies on \\(y=b\\)), or, when \\(r=1\\) and \\(a,b>q\\), we have \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal. Thus all points are covered.\n\n   *Increasing the number of sunny lines while keeping the total at \\(n\\).*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,q\\}\\) replace the pair of lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=i\\quad\\text{and}\\quad y=i\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = m_i(x-i)+i ,\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   where the slope \\(m_i\\) is chosen arbitrarily from \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) and distinct for different \\(i\\).  \n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the intersection point \\((i,i)\\) of the two removed lines, so that point remains covered.  \n   All other points that were on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (resp. horizontal line \\(y=i\\)) are still covered because the remaining vertical lines \\(x\\neq i\\) (resp. horizontal lines