            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{-}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line must have a finite, non‑zero slope different from \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (the case \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider  \n\n\\[\nP_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(\\{P_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) are distinct, each is parallel to \\(\\ell_{-}\\) and therefore **not** sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above range, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(P_c\\).  \nHence the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines \\(P_2,\\dots ,P_{n+1}\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (the case \\(k=n\\))\n\nDefine, for \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x-\\frac{i}{i-1},\n\\]\n\nand let  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\n*Each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.*  \nIndeed, the slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(\\frac{i}{i-1}\\neq 0,-1\\) for \\(i\\ge 2\\); \\(L_1\\) is vertical, hence not parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\) or \\(\\ell_{-}\\).\n\n*The lines are distinct.*  \n\\(L_1\\) is the only vertical line. For \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i-1}\\) are all different, so the lines cannot coincide.\n\n*Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.*  \nFix \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Put \\(s=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nIf \\(s\\le k+1\\) we will later use a sunny line; for the present lemma we have \\(k=n\\), thus \\(s\\le n+1\\).  \nObserve that for each \\(i\\ge 2\\)\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{i-1}a-\\frac{i}{i-1}\n\\iff (i-1)b=i a-i\n\\iff i(a-b)=i .\n\\]\n\nHence the equality holds precisely when \\(a-b=1\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a-b=1\\) are \\((2,1),(3,2),\\dots ,(n+1,n)\\); each of them satisfies the equation of \\(L_{a-1}\\).  \n\nThe remaining points of \\(S_n\\) have \\(a=1\\); they are \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).  \nEach such point lies on the vertical line \\(L_1\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) cover \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\), \\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be fixed.  \n\n*Sunny part.*  \nTake the first \\(k\\) sunny lines from Lemma 2:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_k=\\{L_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i\\ge 2\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i^{(1)}=(i,1),\\qquad P_i^{(2)}=(i+1,2).\n\\]\n\n\\(L_1\\) contains all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nConsequently the set of points covered by \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) is  \n\n\\[\nC_k=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\n      \\bigcup_{i=2}^{k}\\{(i,1),(i+1,2)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nConsider the family of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_k=\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{\\,k+2,\\dots ,k+1+(n-k)\\,\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{k+2,\\dots ,k+1+(n-k)\\}\\) has exactly \\(n-k\\) elements, so \\(|\\mathcal N_k|=n-k\\).  \nEach line in \\(\\mathcal N_k\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{-}\\) and therefore not sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(c=a+b\\) for a unique integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(c\\) belongs to the excluded block \\(\\{k+2,\\dots ,k+1+(n-k)\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) is one of the points listed in (1); indeed the excluded block consists exactly of the sums \\(i+1\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) and of the sums \\(i+2\\) for \\(i=2,\\dots ,k\\), which correspond to the points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\) together with the whole column \\(x=1\\). Hence such a point lies on a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).\n\n* If \\(c\\) is not in the excluded block, then the line \\(x+y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal N_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\cup\\mathcal N_k\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe sunny lines have distinct slopes (or are vertical), so they are pairwise different.  \nThe non‑sunny lines have distinct constants \\(c\\), hence are pairwise different.  \nA sunny line cannot be of the form \\(x+y=c\\) because its slope is never \\(-1\\).  \nTherefore the total collection consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable, Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is attainable, and Lemma 3 proves that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct ste