nny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; y=\\alpha x+\\beta_{i}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t),\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\alpha=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) (so \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\)) and the intercepts are\n   chosen as \\(\\beta_{i}= -\\alpha (i-1)\\).  Thus \\(S_{i}\\) passes through the\n   point \\((i-1,0)\\); the lines are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n   The resulting set of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{t}= \\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{t}\\}\\cup\n   \\{y=t,\\dots ,y=n-3\\}\\cup\\{x=0,\\;x+y=n+1\\},\n   \\]\n   which contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-t\\) non‑sunny lines,\n   altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   – For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le t\\) we have \\(b=\\alpha a+\\beta_{b}\\);\n   solving for \\(a\\) gives a unique integer \\(a\\) (because \\(\\alpha\\) is a\n   rational number with denominator \\(t\\) and \\(a\\le n-t\\) by the condition\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{b}\\).  \n   – Points with \\(t<b\\le n-3\\) are covered by the unchanged horizontal line\n   \\(y=b\\).  \n   – Points with \\(b=n-2\\) or \\(b=n-1\\) are covered as in the \\(k=0\\) case by\n   the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) or the vertical line \\(x=0\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\).  Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines\n   exists for every integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only forbidden value is \\(k=1\\); all other integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are\n   attainable.  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:\\;y=0,\\qquad\n\\ell_{y}:\\;x=0,\\qquad\n\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  No configuration with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of distinct lines satisfies the\ncovering condition and contains exactly one sunny line, say \\(L_{s}\\).\nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are parallel to one of \\(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y},\\ell_{d}\\).\n\nAmong these \\(n-1\\) lines, by the pigeon‑hole principle at least two share the\nsame forbidden direction; denote them \\(M_{1},M_{2}\\).\n\n*Horizontal case.*  \nBoth are of the form \\(y=c_{1}\\) and \\(y=c_{2}\\) with \\(c_{1}\\neq c_{2}\\).\nA point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lying on either line must have second coordinate\n\\(c_{i}\\).  Since \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) for points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\), the two\nhorizontal lines together contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*Vertical case.*  \nAnalogous: two vertical lines \\(x=d_{1},x=d_{2}\\) together contain at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*Diagonal case.*  \nLines are \\(x+y=e_{1}\\) and \\(x+y=e_{2}\\) with \\(e_{1}\\neq e_{2}\\).  For a fixed\nvalue of \\(e\\) the integer solutions of \\(x+y=e\\) with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) are\n\\((1,e-1),(2,e-2),\\dots\\); there are at most \\(e-1\\) of them, and because\n\\(e\\le n+1\\) each diagonal contributes at most \\(n\\) points, so the two\ndiagonals together contribute at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nThus the two parallel non‑sunny lines cover at most \\(n\\) points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\nAny other line (including the unique sunny line) can intersect each of\n\\(M_{1},M_{2}\\) in at most one point, so it can add at most one new point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that is not already on \\(M_{1}\\cup M_{2}\\).  With the remaining\n\\(n-2\\) lines we can therefore cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-2)=2n-2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n\\]\n\npoints, contradicting the covering requirement.  Hence a configuration with\n\\(k=1\\) does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:y=0,\\\\\nL_{2}&:y=1,\\\\\n&\\ \\vdots\\\\\nL_{n-2}&:y=n-3,\\\\\nL_{n-1}&:x=0,\\\\\nL_{n}&:x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll are parallel to \\(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y}\\) or \\(\\ell_{d}\\); none is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-3\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (one of\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\)).\n* If \\(b=n-2\\) then \\(a=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((1,n-2)\\) satisfies\n\\(x+y=n+1\\), i.e. lies on \\(L_{n}\\).\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=0\\), so the point lies on the vertical line\n\\(x=0\\) (i.e. on \\(L_{n-1}\\)).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines,\nand we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Existence for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).  \nFrom the configuration of Section 2 replace the first \\(t\\) horizontal lines\n\\(y=0, y=1,\\dots ,y=t-1\\) by the following \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\nChoose a slope  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha:=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\qquad(\\alpha\\neq0,-1).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\alpha x+\\beta_{i},\\qquad\\beta_{i}:=-\\alpha (i-1).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(S_{i}\\) passes through the point \\((i-1,0)\\); the lines are distinct\nbecause their intercepts \\(\\beta_{i}\\) are distinct.  Since \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\),\neach \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{t}:=\n\\underbrace{\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{t}\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{y=t,\\dots ,y=n-3\\}}_{\\text{horizontal, non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{x=0,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nCounting lines:\n\n\\[\nt\\;+\\;(n-3-(t-1))\\;+\\;2=n,\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which\nprecisely \\(t\\) are sunny.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le b\\le t\\):*  \nFrom the definition of \\(S_{b}\\),\n\n\\[\nb=\\alpha a+\\beta_{b}\n   =\\frac{t-1}{t}a-\\frac{t-1}{t}(b-1)\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   a=\\frac{t}{t-1}\\bigl(b+\\frac{t-1}{t}(b-1)\\bigr)\n   =b-1+(t-b+1)=a,\n\\]\n\nso the equality holds and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{b}\\).  Moreover,\n\\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n-b\\le n-t\\), guaranteeing that the required integer\nsolution exists; consequently every point with \\(b\\le t\\) is covered by the\ncorresponding sunny line.\n\n*If \\(t<b\\le n-3\\):*  \nThe point lies on the unchanged horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belonging to\n\\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\).\n\n*If \\(b=n-2\\) or \\(b=n-1\\):*  \nExactly as in the \\(k=0\\) construction, the point lies on the diagonal line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) or on the vertical line \\(x=0\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is contained in at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\).  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines\nexists for every integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final classification  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* \\(k=1\\) is impossible (Section 1);\n* \\(k=0\\) is possible (Section 2);\n* for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists (Section 3).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
