k,k)=\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+c\\mid c\\in\\{-\\, (n-k),\\dots ,k-(n-k)-1\\}\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the second part), and by the Lemma covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The extremal cases \\(k=0\\) (all vertical) and \\(k=n\\) (all slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines) are obtained as specialisations of the same formula.\n\n5. **Necessity of the bound.**  \n   Since we have only \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable, so the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is both necessary and sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently, the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma  \n\nFor any integers \\(p,q\\ge 0\\) with \\(p+q=n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{p}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le p\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nC_{p,q}= \\{-p,\\,-p+1,\\dots ,\\,q-p-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal S_{q}= \\{\\,\\ell_c : y = x + c \\mid c\\in C_{p,q}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(p,q)=\\mathcal V_{p}\\cup\\mathcal S_{q}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le p\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_{p}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>p\\).  \nHence \\(a\\ge p+1\\). From the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1 = p+q+1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b\\le 