s sunny.\n* \\(k=n\\): \\(\\mathcal F_n = \\{\\,y = x + c \\mid c = -n,\\dots , -1\\}\\) \u2013 all lines have slope \\(1\\), so all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nThese are special instances of the general construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nSince we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; hence \\(k\\le n\\). The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is indeed attainable. Therefore no other non\u2011negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
