htarrow\\quad b\\le n+1-a\\le n-p .\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(c:=b-a\\). Because \\(a\\ge p+1\\) and \\(b\\le n-p\\),\n\n\\[\n-\\,(n-p)\\le b-a = c \\le (n-p)-(p+1)=p-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(c\\in C_{p}\\) and the line \\(\\ell_c\\) (equation \\(y=x+c\\)) belongs to \\(\\mathcal S_{p}\\) and passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn both cases \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L(p)\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal L(p)\\) covers \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a prescribed number \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\np=n-k .\n\\]\n\nConsider the set of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k:=\\mathcal L(p)=\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y = x + c \\mid c\\in\\{-k,\\dots ,n-k-1\\}\\}}_{\\text{sunny}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Properties.*\n\n1. **Number of lines.**  \n   \\(|\\mathcal F_k| = (n-k)+k = n\\).\n\n2. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   The second family consists of lines of slope \\(1\\); none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\). Hence all \\(k\\) of them are sunny, while the first family (vertical lines) is non‑sunny. Consequently \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Coverage of \\(T_n\\).**  \n   By the Lemma with the chosen \\(p=n-k\\), the union \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for each admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both conditions of the problem and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Extremal cases\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (\\(p=n\\)): \\(\\mathcal F_0=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\) – all lines are vertical, none is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=n\\) (\\(p=0\\)): \\(\\mathcal F_n=\\{\\,y = x + c \\mid c\\in\\{-n,\\dots ,-1\\}\\}\\) – all \\(n\\) lines have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny.  \n  The Lemma (with \\(p=0\\)) guarantees that these \\(n\\) lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the bound\n\nSince exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable. Hence no other non‑negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
