            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma  \n\nFor each integer \\(p\\) with \\(0\\le p\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{p}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le p\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nC_{p}= \\{-p,\\,-p+1,\\dots ,\\,n-2p-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal S_{p}= \\{\\,\\ell_c : y = x + c \\mid c\\in C_{p}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(p)=\\mathcal V_{p}\\cup\\mathcal S_{p}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe two families are disjoint (vertical lines have undefined slope, the others have slope \\(1\\)), so  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L(p)|=|\\mathcal V_{p}|+|\\mathcal S_{p}|=p+(n-p)=n .\n\\]\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le p\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_{p}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>p\\).  \nThen \\(a\\ge p+1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nDefine \\(c:=b-a\\). Because \\(a\\ge p+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= b-a \\ge 1-a \\ge 1-(p+1)= -p,\\\\[2pt]\nc &= b-a \\le (n+1-a)-a = n+1-2a \\le n+1-2(p+1)= n-2p-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(-p\\le c\\le n-2p-1\\), i.e. \\(c\\in C_{p}\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(\\ell_c\\in\\mathcal S_{p}\\).\n\nIn both cases \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L(p)\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal L(p)\\) covers \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a prescribed number \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\np=n-k .\n\\]\n\nConsider the set of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k:=\\mathcal L(p)=\n\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+c\\mid c\\in\\{-\\, (n-k),\\dots ,\\,k-2\\}\\}}_{\\text{sunny}} .\n\\]\n\n*Properties.*\n\n1. **Number of lines.**  \n   \\(|\\mathcal F_k|=(n-k)+k=n\\).\n\n2. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   All lines in the second family have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny. Their number is \\(|C_{p}|=k\\). The first family consists of vertical lines, which are not sunny. Thus \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Coverage of \\(T_n\\).**  \n   By the Lemma with \\(p=n-k\\), the set \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence for each admissible \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Extremal cases\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (\\(p=n\\)): \\(\\mathcal F_0=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\) – all lines are vertical, none is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=n\\) (\\(p=0\\)): \\(\\mathcal F_n=\\{\\,y=x+c\\mid c=-n,\\dots , -1\\,\\}\\) – all \\(n\\) lines have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny. The Lemma (with \\(p=0\\)) guarantees that they cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the bound\n\nSince exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are required, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed