oints \\((i,1)\\).  \n   *Contradiction*: The point \\((1,1)\\) also has to lie on one of the lines. If it lies on a line that already contains \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i\\neq 1\\), that line would be horizontal; if it lies on a line that contains a point \\((1,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq 1\\), the line would be vertical; otherwise the line would have slope \\((j-1)/(i-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), which forces the slope to be \\(-1\\) because the only other points of the required set having the same sum of coordinates as \\((1,1)\\) are none. In every case the line is non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. Therefore at least one line is non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k\\neq n\\).\n\n2. **Partial sufficiency** – for the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) explicit constructions are easy (all lines non‑sunny, or all but one line non‑sunny). For intermediate values the problem reduces to arranging a prescribed number of sunny lines while still covering the whole triangular lattice; a full construction has not been completed here.  \n\nThus the only rigorous conclusion is the necessary bound \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
