# 1.  A necessary bound on \\(k\\)\n\n**Lemma 1.**  In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\),\nat least one line is non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points \\((i,1)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n)\\) all belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nIf a line is sunny, it cannot be horizontal; therefore it can contain at most one of the\npoints \\((i,1)\\). Consequently the \\(n\\) points \\((i,1)\\) require at least \\(n\\) distinct lines,\neach of which contains exactly one of them.\n\nConsider the line that contains the point \\((1,1)\\).  \nIf it also contained another point \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i\\neq1\\) it would be horizontal, contradicting\nsunnyness.  \nIf it contained a point \\((1,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq1\\) it would be vertical, also forbidden for a sunny\nline.  \n\nOtherwise the line contains a point \\((p,q)\\) with \\(p\\ge2,\\;q\\ge2\\).  \nIts slope is \\(\\dfrac{q-1}{p-1}>0\\).  A sunny line may have any positive slope, but the line\nthrough \\((1,1)\\) and \\((p,q)\\) would then be parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) **only** when the slope\nequals \\(-1\\), which is impossible because the slope is positive.  Hence the line through\n\\((1,1)\\) cannot be sunny.  ∎\n\nThus any admissible configuration contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince a line can always be chosen to be non‑sunny, the lower bound \\(k\\ge0\\) is trivial.\nHence a necessary condition is  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1.  Non‑sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(k+1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i^{\\text{V}}:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical lines, therefore non‑sunny.  \nAdd the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL^{\\text{H}}:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nAll together we have \\((n-1)-(k+1)+1 = n-k-1\\) vertical lines plus one horizontal line,\ni.e. \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2.  Sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t^{\\text{D}}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{t+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\);\nhence each \\(L_t^{\\text{D}}\\) is sunny.  The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_t^{\\text{D}}\\}_{t=1}^{k}\\) are distinct\nbecause they have different slopes.\n\n#### 2.3.  Verification of the covering property\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b=1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L^{\\text{H}}\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nWrite \\(a=t\\) for some \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).  \nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t}{t+1}x + \\frac{1}{t+1}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a=t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,t + \\frac{1}{t+1}= \\frac{t^2+t+1}{t+1}=t .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((t,t)\\) belongs to \\(L_t^{\\text{D}}\\).  More generally, for any integer\n\\(b\\ge2\\) with \\(a\\equiv 1\\pmod{t+1}\\) the right‑hand side of (1) is an integer equal to \\(b\\);\nsuch points occur precisely when \\(a = (t+1)b - t\\).  Because \\(a\\le k\\le n-1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) indeed lies on the diagonal line \\(L_t^{\\text{D}}\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a>k\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(L_a^{\\text{V}}\\) (which is present because \\(a\\ge k+1\\)) contains the\npoint \\((a,b)\\).\n\nAll three cases exhaust the possibilities, so every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n\\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n#### 2.4.  Count of sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) diagonal lines \\(\\{L_t^{\\text{D}}\\}_{t=1}^{k}\\) are sunny; the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) lines are vertical or horizontal and therefore non‑sunny.  Hence the configuration has\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that no configuration can have \\(k=n\\); therefore \\(k\\le n-1\\) is necessary.  \nThe construction of §2 provides, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nThus the necessary condition is also sufficient.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-1.}\n\\]"
