{D}}\\).  More generally, for any integer\n\\(b\\ge2\\) with \\(a\\equiv 1\\pmod{t+1}\\) the right‑hand side of (1) is an integer equal to \\(b\\);\nsuch points occur precisely when \\(a = (t+1)b - t\\).  Because \\(a\\le k\\le n-1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) indeed lies on the diagonal line \\(L_t^{\\text{D}}\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a>k\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(L_a^{\\text{V}}\\) (which is present because \\(a\\ge k+1\\)) contains the\npoint \\((a,b)\\).\n\nAll three cases exhaust the possibilities, so every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n\\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n#### 2.4.  Count of sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) diagonal lines \\(\\{L_t^{\\text{D}}\\}_{t=1}^{k}\\) are sunny; the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) lines are vertical or horizontal and therefore non‑sunny.  Hence the configuration has\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that no configuration can have \\(k=n\\); therefore \\(k\\le n-1\\) is necessary.  \nThe construction of §2 provides, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nThus the necessary condition is also sufficient.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-1.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
