"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* The \\(n\\) points  \n   \\[\n   A_i=(i,\\,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n   all belong to the required set because \\(i+(n+1-i)=n+1\\).  \n   Any sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(A_i\\): if it contained two, say \\(A_i\\) and \\(A_j\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), then the slope would be\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n+1-j)- (n+1-i)}{j-i}= \\frac{i-j}{j-i}= -1,\n   \\]\n   i.e. the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore not sunny.  \n   Consequently the \\(n\\) points \\(A_1,\\dots ,A_n\\) require at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which contains exactly one of them.\n\n   Suppose, for a contradiction, that **all** of these \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Consider the line \\(\\ell\\) that contains \\(A_1=(1,n)\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\ell\\) cannot be vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\).  \n   The only other points of the required set that lie on \\(\\ell\\) must have a different \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate from \\(1\\) and a different \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate from \\(n\\).  \n   Take any other required point \\(P=(a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n   Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\).  \n   The slope of the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) is\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b-n}{a-1}\\le\\frac{(n-1)-n}{1}= -1 .\n   \\]\n   Equality \\(-1\\) occurs exactly when \\(b=n+1-a\\), i.e. when \\(P=A_a\\).  \n   For any other admissible \\(P\\) the slope is **strictly smaller** than \\(-1\\); in particular it is negative.  \n   Thus every line through \\((1,n)\\) and another required point is either of slope \\(-1\\) (the forbidden case) or has slope \\(< -1\\).  \n   A line