sequently the removal of the vertical line \\(x=i\\) does not leave any point of \\(T_n\\) uncovered.\n\nAfter performing the replacements for all \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (slope \\(\\sigma\\));\n* \\(n-1-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\ge k+1\\);\n* one diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = k+(n-1-k)+1 = n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Verification of the covering property\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge k+1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le a\\le k\\)*, then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(b=n+1-a\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nIf \\(b<n+1-a\\) then \\(a+b<n+1\\); such a point is still on the diagonal because the diagonal contains **all** points with sum \\(n+1\\) and any point with a smaller sum is already covered by a vertical line \\(x\\ge k+1\\) or by the diagonal itself. In either case the point is covered.\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Distinctness of the lines\n\nThe sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) have distinct intercepts (they pass through the distinct points \\((i,n+1-i)\\)), so they are pairwise different.  \nThe remaining vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, and the diagonal line is different from any vertical line. Consequently all \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Fro