*  \nPair the points of \\(P\\) arbitrarily; because the \\(x\\)-coordinates are distinct,\neach pair satisfies the hypothesis of Lemma 1, so each pair determines a sunny\nline.  If \\(|P|\\) is even, all points are covered by \\(|P|/2\\) sunny lines.\nIf \\(|P|\\) is odd, one point remains; it can be covered by a vertical line\n\\(x=c\\) (or a horizontal line), which is non‑sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nAssume \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that\ncover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n1. **Selection of sunny pairs.**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) take the two points  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i,2).\n   \\]  \n   The \\(2k\\) points \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) have pairwise distinct\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates (the numbers \\(1,\\dots ,k\\)) and pairwise distinct\n   \\(y\\)-coordinates (the numbers \\(1\\) and \\(2\\)).  Hence they satisfy the\n   hypothesis of Lemma 2.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\) define the line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\):\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-1=\\frac{2-1}{i-i}\\,(x-i).\n   \\]  \n   Since the two points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, \\(\\ell_i\\) is the\n   vertical line \\(x=i\\).  A vertical line is **not** sunny, so we modify the\n   choice of pairs to avoid equal \\(x\\)-coordinates.  Instead take  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,2)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n   \\]  \n\n   Now the \\(x\\)-coordinates are all distinct, and the \\(y\\)-coordinates are\n   also distinct.  By Lemma 1 each line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) is\n   sunny.  These \\(k\\) lines are the required sunny lines.\n\n3. **Remaining points.**  \n   Let  \n   \\[\n   R=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i\\}.\n   \\]  \n   The set \\(R\\) contains \\(|S_n|-2k\\) points.  All points of \\(R\\) have\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates belonging to the set\n   \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i,i+1\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\); there are at most\n   \\(n-2k\\) distinct such \\(x\\)-coordinates.\n\n   For each distinct \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(c\\) occurring in \\(R\\) introduce the\n   vertical line \\(x=c\\).  These lines are non‑sunny and together cover every\n   point of \\(R\\).  The number of vertical lines used is at most \\(n-2k\\).\n\n4. **Counting the lines.**  \n   We have produced  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2k)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;=\\,n-k\n   \\]  \n\n   distinct lines.  If \\(n-k<n\\) we add any \\(k\\) further vertical lines with\n   constants not used before (for instance \\(x=n+1,\\dots ,n+k\\)).  Adding these\n   lines does not affect the covering property because they contain no points of\n   \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Consequently we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the\n   first \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) the required\nconfiguration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that all integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nare admissible.  The converse (that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible) has\nnot been established; therefore the full classification of admissible \\(k\\) is\nstill open. ∎"
